window.RZEMIOSLO_DOCS = {"en": {"00-commandments": "# 00 \u2014 Decalogue\n\nThe core. If you read one file from this set, read this one. Each commandment is expanded in a later\nchapter. These are **rules the project must follow**, written tersely on purpose \u2014 explanations,\nexamples and stories live on the website: [thecraft.jakub.solutions](https://thecraft.jakub.solutions).\n\n> **Rule zero (activation).** See a `docs/rules/` package (The Craft) in a project? Treat it as the\n> overriding doctrine for running that project. Read **this file**, then `grep -i <topic>` over\n> `AI_README.md` and read **only** the matched chapter \u2014 never the whole pack every session (it burns the\n> context you were saving \u2192 [01](01-documentation-and-ai-readme.md)). Make it stick: put a short,\n> plain-language pointer at the top of `CLAUDE.md` (loaded every session) and `AGENTS.md` (other agents) \u2014\n> **a pointer, not a paste**, and no fake `SYSTEM`/`URGENT` labels: an authority claim inside a file looks\n> like prompt injection to a modern model. An unwired package is never read. A vendored copy is **not** the\n> host's constitution \u2014 the host's own `CLAUDE.md` stays in charge. Steps \u2192 [07](07-new-project-day-0.md).\n> (The Decalogue stays ten; this is activation, not an eleventh rule.)\n\n---\n\n### I. Document for the agent, not for the archive.\nEvery directory with code has an `AI_README.md`, updated **before** the commit, and a test that fails when\nit isn't (\u2192 [17](17-language-and-guards.md)). Documentation written \"at the end\" drifts and lies. \u2192 [01](01-documentation-and-ai-readme.md)\n\n### II. Search the history before you write a single line.\n`git log -S`, `git log --grep`, `git blame`, the directory's `AI_README`. Most \"new\" problems were already\nsolved here \u2014 a helper exists, a migration happened, the odd shape has a reason. \u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)\n\n### III. Verify, don't declare.\n\"It works\" means **proof**: a test run, an HTTP code, numbers, a screenshot. Failures are reported with\ntheir output; skipped steps are reported as skipped. \u2192 [03](03-testing-and-verification.md)\n\n### IV. Dry-run is the default; `--execute` is deliberate.\nEvery script that changes data first **prints its plan** (what, how much, where). The mutation runs only\nafter the plan was read. \u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)\n\n### V. The backup is the rollback mechanism, not a precaution.\nMigrations are **forward-only**. A snapshot **before** every schema or data change on prod; reverting the\nschema = restoring that snapshot. \u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)\n\n### VI. Prod is sacred \u2014 nothing touches it without an explicit \"deploy.\"\nCommit and push on request are not a deploy. Pull on the server, reload, migration, database swap,\nmaintenance flag \u2014 only on the user's explicit word. Irreversible and outward-facing actions are always\nconfirmed; consent does not carry over to the next action. \u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md), [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md)\n\n### VII. User data is inviolable.\nNever lose it, never leak it, never mutate it without a backup. Reference it by a **stable key** (slug),\nnever by an ID that drifts; a missing match is skipped and logged, not forced. After any operation on it,\ncount the rows and run the integrity check. Full prod database swap \u2192 [19](19-database-swap-runbook.md).\n\n### VIII. Tag every deploy and tell the user what they gained.\nAn annotated tag (`deploy-YYYY-MM-DD`) is the only stable marker of \"what's live\" and the basis for\nrollback. Every deploy updates the public changelog in plain language \u2014 what the user gains, no jargon. \u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)\n\n### IX. Small, coherent commit; one topic.\nUnrelated changes go to separate commits. Keep `git status` clean: catch junk (`.bak`, temp files, stray\ndatabases) and orphans (someone else's uncommitted work) **early**. \u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)\n\n### X. Plan \u2192 iterate \u2192 review.\nA plan or a sketch first (3 examples, not 100), feedback, then scale. Deliver UX with **warmth**, not just\ncorrectness \u2014 the product should be pleasant, not merely functional. \u2192 [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md)\n\n---\n\n## Seven deadly sins\n\n1. **Inflated optimism** \u2014 \"everything works\" with no proof. (III)\n2. **Mutation without a plan** \u2014 a data-changing script run straight with `--execute`. (IV)\n3. **Auto-deploy** \u2014 \"it's ready, so it goes to prod.\" (VI)\n4. **User data on a hunch** \u2014 mapping by ID, or a swap without enumerating every table that points at the user. (VII)\n5. **Junk-drawer commit** \u2014 unrelated changes and artifacts in one commit. (IX)\n6. **Scaling before review** \u2014 500 generated before the user saw 3. (X)\n7. **Documentation \"later\"** \u2014 the code ships, the `AI_README` lags, the next session wanders. (I)\n\n---\n\n## The golden rule of altitude\n\n> **Slow where a mistake is expensive (prod, user data, schema). Fast where it's cheap (local experiment,\n> UI sketch, dry-run).** Match the pace to the cost of error, not to impatience.\n\n---\n\n## Departing from a rule \u2014 and the floor that doesn't move\n\nThis is a default, not a cage. A doctrine that can't bend gets ignored wholesale.\n\n- **Deviate out loud:** name the rule, say why it doesn't fit *here*, propose the alternative, get a yes,\n  record the decision (ADR / `CLAUDE.md`). **Silently skipping a rule is the sin, not a deviation.**\n- **Precedence:** the host project's root `CLAUDE.md` > this doctrine (`docs/rules/`) > the agent's defaults.\n- **A rule you keep overriding is a bug in the doctrine** \u2014 fix it at the source (the canon, with its\n  translation), don't route around it every session.\n\n> **The floor \u2014 not negotiable:** honest reporting (**III**), a backup before a schema/data change (**V**),\n> an explicit \"deploy\" before prod (**VI**), user data (**VII**). The full boundary as one table \u2192 [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md).\n", "01-documentation-and-ai-readme": "# 01 \u2014 Documentation and AI_README\n\n> Commandment I: *document for the agent, not for the archive.* The goal: the next session understands a\n> directory without reading all of its code.\n\n## Three layers, split by reader\n\n| Layer | File | Holds |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Constitution** | `CLAUDE.md` (root) | what the project is, exact run commands, **policies that override default behavior**, a dated \"Current state\" with numbers. Loaded every session. |\n| **Directory map** | `AI_README.md` in every directory with code | module index, key APIs (signature + contract), gotchas, numbers, the grep index. Read **before** touching that directory. |\n| **Reference / plans** | `docs/` | architecture, data model, runbooks, `plans/`. |\n\n`README.md` is the public front door (what it is, how to start) \u2014 not a layer for the agent. **Where does a\nfact go? Ask who reads it** \u2014 and write it once; the other files link.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **`CLAUDE.md` points to the `AI_README.md` maps** \u2014 without that pointer a new session doesn't know they exist.\n2. **`AI_README.md` is updated before the commit**, in the same commit as the code. A test enforces the\n   index (every module named, size ceiling) \u2192 [17](17-language-and-guards.md).\n3. **Write the index for `grep`, not for reading.** Keyword-dense one-liners: concrete names, synonyms, both\n   languages if the project is bilingual. `grep -i <topic>` should hit **one line** pointing at **one file**.\n4. **One file = one topic.** A topic spread over five files is five hits and burned context.\n5. **State, not intent.** Describe what the code does now; delete entries for removed code \u2014 a dead entry is\n   worse than none. Numbers (records, tests) go stale \u2192 update them.\n6. **`docs/` describes the current version only.** History lives in git and the `CHANGELOG`; update the doc,\n   don't keep the old version beside it.\n7. **`docs/plans/` is a living backlog.** Problem \u2192 options \u2192 choice \u2192 why. When a plan lands, fold the\n   decision into the doc / `CLAUDE.md` it governs and **delete the plan** \u2014 git keeps the record.\n8. **A new directory is created together with its `AI_README.md`** \u2014 never \"later\".\n9. **Generated artifacts (HTML, snapshots) are derivatives:** commit them, never edit them by hand,\n   regenerate on command (a skill), not on every commit.\n\nUpdate the docs for: a new module/function/script/flag, a schema change, a behavior change (threshold,\nfallback), changed counts. Not for: a pure bugfix with no API change, typos.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab \"I'll update the docs at the end\" \u2014 the end never comes, the docs lie.\n- \ud83d\udeab An `AI_README` describing wishes instead of state.\n- \ud83d\udeab The same state copied into five files \u2014 one source of truth, the rest link.\n- \ud83d\udeab Generic descriptions (\"helpers\", \"various rules\") \u2014 grep misses, the agent reads everything.\n", "02-skills-and-refactoring": "# 02 \u2014 Skills and refactoring\n\n> Commandments I and X: automate what repeats; refactor so the code reads like its neighbor.\n\n## Skills (slash-commands)\n\n1. **Build a skill when** a procedure repeats (\u22653 times), its order is easy to get wrong (migration:\n   number \u2192 SQL \u2192 apply \u2192 `AI_README` \u2192 commit), or it carries a project-specific gotcha\n   (`PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8` on Windows, `--runInBand` while a server runs).\n2. **A skill has an output contract** (exit 0 = green, otherwise what failed) and **prints anything\n   destructive** it does.\n3. **A skill is a recipe for the agent; a hook is automation run by the harness.** \"Always do X after Y\" =\n   a hook in config, not a skill and not a memory.\n4. Starter set: `/run` (server + smoke), `/run-tests`, `/update-ai-readme`, `/add-migration` (relational DB).\n\n## Refactoring\n\n1. **Search for prior art first** (`git log -S`, `git blame`) \u2014 an \"ugly\" shape often has a reason.\n2. **Refactor separately from a behavior change** \u2014 one commit is either cleanup or a feature.\n3. **Reuse before rewrite** \u2014 check whether the helper already exists.\n4. **Keep old entry points working** on structural moves (re-exporting shims), or update every caller in the same change.\n5. **Small verified steps:** refactor \u2192 tests green \u2192 commit.\n6. **Apply SOLID proportionally:** one reason to change per module; extend behind a flag rather than editing a\n   hot path (\u2192 [12](12-flexibility-and-scalability.md)); **no abstraction before the second concrete case.**\n7. **Two review modes, never mixed:** simplification (reuse, clarity) vs correctness (adversarial bug-hunt).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab A skill for something done once.\n- \ud83d\udeab A skill that silently runs something destructive.\n- \ud83d\udeab A big-bang rewrite after which nobody can say what broke.\n- \ud83d\udeab Five layers and a factory for a three-field CRUD.\n", "03-testing-and-verification": "# 03 \u2014 Testing and verification\n\n> Commandment III: *verify, don't declare.* Tests are the net you run again and again; verification is\n> observed proof that **this** change does what it should. Both are mandatory.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Every commit that changes behavior carries a test.** New endpoint \u2192 route test; new threshold or\n   fallback \u2192 threshold test; fixed bug \u2192 a test that reproduced it first. A behavior change without a test\n   is an **incomplete** commit. TDD (red \u2192 green \u2192 refactor) is the default mode.\n2. **Show proof, don't ask the user to check:** HTTP code / redirect, a confirming HTML fragment, numbers\n   from the database (`integrity_check: ok`), a screenshot for visual changes.\n3. **Smoke tests check the right page, not just `200`** \u2014 a marker proving the expected content rendered.\n   Test auth from both sides: anonymous is bounced, logged-in sees the content.\n4. **After a deploy, smoke the live server** (via `localhost` behind the maintenance flag).\n5. **Report honestly:** failures with their output; skipped steps as skipped; a failure caused by data drift\n   (stale fixture) named as such, with a follow-up \u2014 not hidden, not used as a blocker.\n6. **Numbers come from the source**, not from memory or docs: `SELECT COUNT(*)` doesn't lie.\n7. **A guard is proven by the incident, not by the fixture.** A check written after an outage is replayed\n   against that outage's **real data** before it's trusted \u2014 fixtures share the author's blind spot (a\n   freshness check once passed nine green tests and missed its own outage: `>` where `>=` was needed). Then\n   freeze the boundary in a fixture, with a test **on** the boundary.\n8. **Test the contract, not the implementation; keep the suite fast and deterministic** (isolate I/O, seed\n   randomness, serialize tests that share a port/DB). **CI gates** merge and deploy.\n9. **Web projects carry by default:** per-page SEO meta, canonical + hreflang, JSON-LD, colour contrast and\n   keyboard navigation, **language parity as a failing test**, no dead internal links.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab \"Should work\" as a conclusion.\n- \ud83d\udeab One test run, \"suite green\" declared.\n- \ud83d\udeab No smoke after deploy \"because the tests passed\".\n- \ud83d\udeab \"It compiles\" confused with \"it works for the user\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Weakening a test or guard to make it green.\n", "04-scripts-and-databases": "# 04 \u2014 Scripts and databases\n\n> Commandments IV and V: *dry-run is the default; the backup is the rollback.*\n\n## Scripts\n\n1. **Dry-run by default.** A data-changing script prints the plan (what, how many rows, where); a deliberate\n   `--execute` writes.\n2. **Idempotent.** Running it twice yields the same state (`INSERT OR IGNORE` / `UPSERT`).\n3. **Print what you did *not* do** \u2014 truncation, sampling, skipped rows. Silence reads as \"covered everything\".\n4. **Catalog-mutating operations stay out of automatic pipelines** (dedup, merge): dry-run \u2192 review \u2192 `--execute` by hand.\n5. **The environment is written in `CLAUDE.md`** \u2014 interpreter, variables, venv. The agent doesn't guess.\n6. **Absolute paths when a value crosses tools.** `/tmp` means one place to Git Bash, another to Python on\n   Windows, a third to PowerShell \u2014 each tool then reads a *different file*. Pass fully-qualified paths; when\n   a run \"did nothing\", print `os.path.abspath(p)` / `pwd -W` and compare.\n\n## Databases and migrations\n\n1. **Forward-only migrations.** No down-migrations; rolling back the schema = restoring the backup.\n2. **Back up before every schema/data change on prod**, with a timestamp and a retention window:\n   `sqlite3 data/app.db \".backup backups/pre-deploy-$(date +%F_%H%M%S).db\"`\n3. **Snapshot with `.backup` / `VACUUM INTO`, never `cp`/`scp` of a live database** \u2014 WAL makes the copy inconsistent.\n4. **Additive before destructive.** `ADD COLUMN` is safe; `DROP`/`RENAME` waits for a later deploy, once no\n   running code reads the column.\n5. **Check integrity after every data operation:** `integrity_check`, `foreign_key_check`, row counts. Tell\n   pre-existing noise (present before and after) from a regression (new violations).\n6. **Rehearse a bulk or irreversible mutation on a fresh prod copy** \u2014 a dry-run shows the plan, not the\n   outcome. `.backup` prod \u2192 run on the copy \u2192 check counts, integrity and the tricky cases \u2192 then prod.\n   The deploy-level sibling of this rule \u2192 [18](18-release-rehearsal.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab A mutating script without `--dry-run`.\n- \ud83d\udeab A migration deployed without a backup \"because it's additive\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Dropping a column running code still reads.\n- \ud83d\udeab `cp app.db` while the server runs.\n- \ud83d\udeab A catalog-mutating step wired into the automatic pipeline.\n", "05-git-and-deployments": "# 05 \u2014 Git and deployments\n\n> Commandments II, VI, VIII, IX. Git is the project's memory and the undo button for everything else;\n> the deploy policy is where a mistake costs the most. Rehearsal on preprod \u2192 [18](18-release-rehearsal.md);\n> prod database swap \u2192 [19](19-database-swap-runbook.md).\n\n## Git habits\n\n1. **Search the history before you implement:**\n   ```bash\n   git log -S\"symbol\" --oneline     # where a symbol/flag/column was born\n   git log -G\"regex\" --oneline      # commits whose diff matches\n   git log --grep=\"word\" --oneline  # commit messages\n   git blame <file> -L a,b          # who/when/why\n   ```\n2. **Keep `git status` clean.** One topic per commit. Junk (`.bak`, temp files, stray databases) goes to\n   `.gitignore` early (`#` comments on their own line). A file shown as changed with an empty\n   `git diff HEAD` is CRLF noise, not work \u2014 don't commit it.\n3. **Orphans:** uncommitted work you didn't write (another session's) is **never swept into your commit or\n   deploy** without confirmation \u2014 ask whether it's finished work or an experiment.\n4. **Commit messages in English**, what + why, `refs #N` / `fixes #N`.\n\n## Undo: you probably didn't lose it\n\nCommitted work is recoverable; git remembers every position a branch held (~90 days). **Commit early** \u2014 a\ncommit is free, private until pushed, and is your save point.\n\n| Situation | Way back |\n|---|---|\n| Wrecked a file, not committed | `git restore <file>` |\n| Undo the last commit, keep the work | `git reset --soft HEAD~1` |\n| Committed on the wrong branch | `git branch <right>` here, then `git reset --hard HEAD~1` on the wrong one |\n| Deleted a branch / \"lost\" commits | `git reflog` \u2192 find the hash \u2192 `git branch <name> <hash>` |\n| A merge went wrong (not pushed) | `git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD` |\n| Need a file as it was | `git show <commit>:<path>` \u00b7 `git restore --source=<commit> -- <path>` |\n\nOnly three commands destroy work \u2014 and only **uncommitted** work: `git reset --hard`, `git restore .` /\n`git checkout .`, `git clean -fd`. Git has no dry-run for them: **committing first is the dry-run** (\u2192 IV).\n\n## Parallel work: issues and short branches\n\n\"Solo\" is still multi-writer: one developer routinely runs **several Claude sessions** on one repo. The\n\"other party\" is usually another session \u2014 read \"teammate\" as \"the other session\".\n\n1. **One task = one issue**: title = outcome, body = context + acceptance criteria. The issue number ties\n   branch, commits and PR together; the **PR/merge** closes it, not a work-branch commit.\n2. **Short branches** (`feat/NN-cart`, `fix/NN-login`) living hours\u2013days; small PRs; merge, delete, sync\n   with `main` often.\n3. **Diff before merging into the deploy branch** \u2014 a merge ships *every* commit on the source, including\n   other sessions' work: `git log <deploy>..<source> --oneline`. An urgent isolated fix is **cherry-picked**.\n4. **After any cherry-pick, verify the result:** `git diff <deploy-base> HEAD --stat` must show only your\n   change \u2014 a 3-way fallback can drag in a neighbour's hunk (a stray `require` \u2192 boot crash \u2192 502).\n5. **One concurrent session = one `git worktree`**, never two sessions in one checkout (\u2192 [16](16-driving-claude.md)).\n\n**Branching grows with the project \u2014 add each rung when a metric forces it:**\n1. Solo, no live users \u2192 just `main`, committed to with discipline.\n2. First production deploy \u2192 every change on a short branch, reviewed, merged to an always-deployable `main`, deploy tagged.\n3. More people / parallel streams \u2192 `develop` for integration, releases `develop \u2192 main`, verified on a\n   preprod copy of prod first (\u2192 [18](18-release-rehearsal.md)).\n\nEvery change reaches `main` reviewed \u2014 solo, the reviewer is a deliberate second pass over the diff.\n\n## Deployments \u2014 the overriding policy\n\n> \u26d4 **Never deploy to prod automatically.** Pull on the server, reload, migration, database swap, the\n> maintenance flag \u2014 **only on the user's explicit \"deploy\".** You may propose a deploy; you wait for the\n> yes. Commit and push are not a deploy. This rule beats any auto mode.\n\n1. **The server deploys `origin/<branch>`, not your laptop.** Before every deploy: `git fetch`, then\n   `git log origin/<b>..<b>` (unpushed commits \u2192 push first) and `git log <b>..origin/<b>` (another session\n   pushed \u2192 pull first).\n2. **Boot-check on the target before reloading:** after the pull, start the new code in a throwaway process\n   (`node -e \"require('./src/app')\"`, with `listen` behind `require.main === module`); reload **only** on\n   its OK marker. A crash then leaves the old process serving.\n3. **Two deploy types.** Code-only \u2192 pull + boot-check + zero-downtime reload. Migration / dependency change\n   / database swap \u2192 a **maintenance window** (branded 503 page) so nothing runs on a half-migrated schema.\n4. **Tag every deploy:** `git tag -a deploy-$(date +%F) -m \"<what goes live>\"` + `git push --tags`.\n   What's live: `git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match 'deploy-*'`. Code rollback = checkout the tag +\n   reload; schema rollback = restore the backup.\n5. **Every deploy updates the public changelog** in plain language \u2014 what the user gains.\n6. **Every prod incident becomes a dated, numbered lesson in the runbook** (`docs/`), read before the next\n   deploy (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n7. **First real users = a nightly database backup, deploy tags and a branded maintenance page \u2014 all on day one of traffic.**\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Auto-deploy, \"it's ready so it goes\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Deploying without reconciling local \u21c4 `origin` \u2014 unpushed commits silently don't ship.\n- \ud83d\udeab Merging a shared branch into the deploy branch without reading what it brings.\n- \ud83d\udeab A long-lived \"big refactor\" branch; an \"everything at once\" PR.\n- \ud83d\udeab Working without an issue \u2014 the \"why\" is gone a month later.\n- \ud83d\udeab No deploy tag \u2014 \"what's live?\" becomes guesswork.\n- \ud83d\udeab A deploy-script invariant with no test (`$(date)` expanded once \u2192 every backup overwrites the same file).\n", "06-collaboration-and-memory": "# 06 \u2014 Collaboration and memory\n\n> Commandments VI and X: how the user and the agent work together, where the boundary runs, and how\n> nothing is lost between sessions.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Plan \u2192 sketch \u2192 scale.** Show 3 examples, gather feedback, then make 10, then 100 \u2014 never the reverse.\n2. **Speak the user's native language** and record it (and their technical level) in `CLAUDE.md` once\n   (\u2192 [07](07-new-project-day-0.md)). Code and commit messages stay English; names a tool prints stay English\n   in any language (\u2192 [17](17-language-and-guards.md)).\n3. **Look at the target before deleting or overwriting.** If it contradicts the description or you didn't\n   create it \u2014 report, don't delete.\n4. **Consent is per action.** \"Deploy X\" is not \"deploy whenever\". Anything published may stay cached or\n   indexed after deletion.\n5. **Memory holds only what the repo can't** \u2014 who the user is, their feedback with the why, constraints\n   not visible in code. Use the agent's built-in memory; never copy repo facts into it.\n\n## The permission map\n\nThe whole boundary in one place \u2014 approved once on Day 0, read every session:\n\n| \u2705 Free \u2014 just do it | \u23f8\ufe0f Ask, wait for a \"yes\" | \u26d4 Never \u2014 whoever asks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Read/grep anything in the repo | Deploy to prod, in any form (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)) | Invent legal/identity data \u2014 tax ID, address, company name (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)) |\n| Run tests, linters, builds | Push/publish to a remote | Declare a practice the code doesn't implement (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)) |\n| Commit locally, often, small | Migration or DB swap on prod (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)) | Commit secrets (\u2192 [08](08-stack-and-technologies.md)) |\n| Create/delete **your own** branches | Send mail/messages to real people | Delete a backup that is somebody's rollback (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)) |\n| Run any script in **dry-run** | Delete/overwrite what you didn't create | Weaken a test or guard to make it green (\u2192 [03](03-testing-and-verification.md)) |\n| Write/update docs, `AI_README`, plans | Add a paid service or dependency (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)) | Report \"it works\" without proof (\u2192 [00](00-commandments.md) III) |\n| Refactor inside the agreed scope | Change scheduler/cron or server config (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)) | Sweep another session's uncommitted work into a commit (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)) |\n| Long jobs in the background, locally | Touch real user data (\u2192 [00](00-commandments.md) VII) | Deploy a migration with no backup taken (\u2192 [00](00-commandments.md) V) |\n\nThe left column is **deliberately wide** \u2014 that is what makes autopilot safe instead of rubber-stamped. A yes\nin the middle column is spent on that one action. The right column doesn't move: \"the user asked\" is not an\nargument there \u2014 say why, offer the honest alternative.\n\n## Close a unit of work with a summary\n\nA scannable summary, not prose \u2014 ideally a widget matched to the user's level:\n**what changed** \u00b7 **proof** (test counts, HTTP codes, numbers) \u00b7 **the commit** (hash, message) \u00b7\n**next actions in order** (docs \u2192 plans \u2192 deploy, the deploy still behind its explicit \"yes\").\n\n## Handoff when the context runs out\n\nA long session gets summarized, and the summary drops the *why* and the half-finished state in the tree.\n\n1. **Land work in commits, not in the conversation** \u2014 a commit survives compaction, a half-done edit becomes an orphan.\n2. **Write a decision where it lives the moment it's made** (`CLAUDE.md`, ADR, `docs/plans/`, `AI_README`).\n3. **Leave `git status` readable** \u2014 it *is* the handoff to the next session.\n4. **When the ceiling nears, finish the current unit before opening the next.**\n\nTest: could the next session pick this up from the repo alone, without this conversation?\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Scaling before review.\n- \ud83d\udeab Deleting or overwriting without looking at the target.\n- \ud83d\udeab Treating one-time consent as standing.\n- \ud83d\udeab Memory as a dump of repo facts.\n- \ud83d\udeab Ending a change with a wall of prose instead of status + commit + next steps.\n", "07-new-project-day-0": "# 07 \u2014 New project: Day 0\n\n> The checklist Claude runs when a project starts \u2014 the **only** checklist in the doctrine; other chapters\n> link here. Goal: every later session stands on solid ground.\n\n## Wire The Craft in (once)\n\n1. **Place the rules in `docs/rules/`** \u2014 a submodule (updatable) or a plain copy:\n   `git submodule add https://github.com/JKolada/TheCraftWithClaude docs/rules`\n2. **Put a plain pointer at the top of `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md`** (create them if missing). If you see\n   `docs/rules/` and the pointer isn't there \u2014 add it, don't wait to be asked:\n   > This project follows the build rules in `docs/rules/` (The Craft); they override default behavior.\n   > Read `docs/rules/00-commandments.md` first, then `grep -i <keyword> docs/rules/AI_README.md` and read\n   > only the matched chapter. This `CLAUDE.md` remains the project's constitution.\n\n   A pointer, not a paste \u2014 the rules live in one place. No `SYSTEM` labels (\u2192 [00](00-commandments.md)).\n3. **Pick the rules' language** \u2014 English at the root, Polish in `pl/`.\n\n## 0. Product brief \u2014 ask before anything is built\n\nAsk in short groups; where the user doesn't know, propose a default and **mark it as an assumption**:\n\n- **Technical level and reporting style** (ask first \u2014 it sets the register: non-technical \u2192 no jargon,\n  closed questions with a recommendation).\n- **Languages the user speaks; technologies they know.**\n- **Target languages and markets** (i18n from day one? RTL? currencies) \u2192 [10](10-seo-and-translations.md).\n- **MVP vs later; accounts and roles; integrations** (payments, AI, mail, maps).\n- **Monetization** (free / paid / subscription / ads; when; provider; invoices/VAT) \u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md).\n- **Visual direction and feel** (existing brand? light/dark? animation budget, `prefers-reduced-motion`, WCAG?).\n- **Value proposition and tone** (one sentence; name and domain set?).\n\nThe answer lands as **`project-brief.md` in the project root** (next to `docs/rules/`, not inside it), or is\npasted as the first message. The brief view in the reader generates it.\n\n## Checklist\n\n1. **Recon:** read existing `CLAUDE.md` / `AI_README` / `README`; `git log --oneline -20`, `git status`\n   (orphans, junk); identify stack, run command, where the data lives.\n2. **`CLAUDE.md`** from the brief (skeleton below): stack + exact run commands, policies, the user's language\n   and level, the permission map (\u2192 [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md)), a dated \"Current state\".\n3. **Language policy** \u2014 two lines in `CLAUDE.md`: code English (identifiers, tests, DB, CLI, keys), prose in\n   the project's language (\u2192 [17](17-language-and-guards.md)).\n4. **Repo hygiene:** `.gitignore` (deps, local DBs, `*.bak`, generated, temp); commit and deploy-tag convention;\n   tasks in GitHub Issues / `docs/plans/`.\n5. **`AI_README.md` in every directory with code**; `docs/` for architecture and data model.\n6. **Tests:** a test skeleton + a smoke test with markers; Playwright on a separate port if there's a UI;\n   **both guards from [17](17-language-and-guards.md) in the first commit**, baseline committed.\n7. **Skills:** `/run` + smoke, `/run-tests`, `/update-ai-readme`, `/add-migration` (\u2192 [02](02-skills-and-refactoring.md)).\n8. **Data:** forward-only migrations, a `backups/` directory with retention, dry-run by default (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n9. **Before the first real user:** global error handler and a 500 that leaks nothing, timeouts on every\n   external call, quotas on paid endpoints, a durable session store in its own file (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md));\n   AI calls with an output contract and a cache (\u2192 [15](15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots.md)).\n10. **Production (prepared, not fired):** deploy runbook, branded maintenance page, rollback plan (tag +\n    backup), public changelog, nightly backup from the first real user (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n11. **Legal:** terms, privacy policy and disclaimers with `[to be filled]` placeholders; consent before\n    trackers (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n12. **Baselines:** Lighthouse after the first working page; indexes on filter columns from the first migration (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)).\n13. **Architecture decisions** only where you depart from the default stack \u2014 one ADR each (\u2192 [08](08-stack-and-technologies.md), [12](12-flexibility-and-scalability.md)).\n\n**Minimal Day 0:** brief \u2192 `CLAUDE.md` \u2192 `.gitignore` \u2192 `/run` with a smoke test \u2192 test skeleton with the\nguards \u2192 `backups/`. The rest grows with the project.\n\n## `CLAUDE.md` skeleton\n\n```\n# CLAUDE.md \u2014 <Project>\n<pointer to docs/rules/ \u2014 see above>\n\n## What this is\n<one paragraph: what it does, for whom>\n\n## Stack & how to run\n<layer \u2192 tech \u2192 location>\nrun: <exact command>\n\n## Policies (override default behavior)\n- Never deploy/push without an explicit \"deploy\". Commit freely; confirm the irreversible (permission map: docs/rules/06).\n- Dry-run by default. Prod is sacred; user data is inviolable.\n- Talk to me in <language>; I know <languages / technologies>.\n- Code: English (identifiers, tests, DB, CLI, keys). Prose: <language>.\n- <project-specific hard rules: privacy, domain constraints>\n\n## Current state (<date>)\n- <counts, migrations applied, what's live>\n```\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Code before the brief \u2014 language, monetization and brand added later are a rewrite.\n- \ud83d\udeab Thirty questions at once \u2014 ask what changes the plan, propose the rest as defaults.\n- \ud83d\udeab Silent assumptions (\"probably Polish only\", \"probably free\").\n- \ud83d\udeab Jargon aimed at a non-technical user.\n", "08-stack-and-technologies": "# 08 \u2014 Stack and technologies\n\n> The golden rule of altitude applied to tools: *the simplest thing that meets today's requirement*; add\n> complexity when a metric forces it (\u2192 [12](12-flexibility-and-scalability.md)).\n\n## Defaults (depart deliberately, with an ADR)\n\n| Layer | Default | Switch when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agent / process | **Claude Code** + **Git** + **GitHub** (repo, PRs, Issues) | \u2014 |\n| Backend, scripts, data | **Python** (FastAPI for an API, pytest) \u2014 the widest ecosystem for scripts, data and AI glue | the job is done better elsewhere: **Go** (concurrent network services, one static binary), **Rust** (hot paths, memory safety, small footprint), **Kotlin** (JVM libraries, Android), **TypeScript/Node** (the web layer is already JS) |\n| Database | **SQLite** (WAL) | concurrent writes, roles, heavy relations \u2192 **PostgreSQL** |\n| Web / front | server-rendered HTML + a little vanilla JS | genuinely rich interaction \u2192 a framework (e.g. Next.js) |\n| API | REST/JSON with an explicit contract (OpenAPI) | \u2014 |\n| Cache / queue | none | a measured traffic profile \u2192 Redis |\n| Packaging | Docker where dependencies hurt | a dependency-free script \u2192 no container |\n| Hosting | **one VPS** (e.g. Hetzner) + nginx + `systemd`/`pm2` \u2014 a **monolith** | uneven/global traffic, cold start acceptable \u2192 serverless |\n| Icons | **Lucide only** (one geometry, one sprite) | \u2014 |\n| LLM | Claude via the official SDK, streamed (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)) | \u2014 |\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **The monolith is the default architecture:** one app, one database, one server \u2014 one deploy, one log,\n   one backup, one rollback. Split into services only when traffic, team or failure isolation demands it.\n2. **The language is chosen by fit, not by habit.** Python is the default, not a dogma: switch when\n   efficiency, concurrency, deployability or a library that only exists in another ecosystem decides \u2014 and\n   say which, with a number or the missing library, in an ADR. One language per layer, not one per mood; a\n   rewrite \"for speed\" needs a measurement first (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)).\n3. **Testable from the first line beats trendy.** A technology you can't cover with fast, deterministic\n   tests is the wrong choice (\u2192 [03](03-testing-and-verification.md)).\n4. **Boring and mature over new:** ecosystem, documentation and testability decide, not hype.\n5. **Reproducible environments:** pinned dependencies (lock file), a virtual environment / image.\n6. **Secrets live in env or a secret store \u2014 never in the repo.**\n7. **Every non-default choice gets an ADR:** the why and the rejected alternatives, in `docs/`.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Microservices, Kubernetes or a distributed database for an MVP.\n- \ud83d\udeab A stack chosen for the CV.\n- \ud83d\udeab A heavy SPA where server-rendered HTML would do.\n- \ud83d\udeab Mixing icon libraries.\n- \ud83d\udeab Unpinned dependencies \u2014 \"it worked yesterday\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Python forced onto a job it serves badly (a hot path, a concurrent service, a library that lives on the JVM) \u2014 or a rewrite in a \"faster\" language with no measurement behind it.\n", "09-law-and-protecting-the-creator": "# 09 \u2014 Law and protecting the creator\n\n> Commandments VI and VII extended outward: a solo creator or sole trader shipping a public app is\n> **personally liable**. Terms, privacy policy and disclaimers are their armor \u2014 written as carefully as a\n> prod migration. \u26a0\ufe0f Engineering hygiene, not legal advice: get a lawyer before binding real users at scale.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Never invent legal or identity data.** Company name, tax ID, address = `[to be filled]` placeholders\n   the owner completes. A visible placeholder beats a plausible fake.\n2. **Never declare a practice the code doesn't implement.** \"We anonymize\", \"we delete after N days\", \"we\n   don't read conversations\" must be true in the code \u2014 ideally proven by a test (\u2192 [11](11-data-model-and-normalization.md)).\n3. **One operator, named identically everywhere:** terms, policy, footer, invoices, UI.\n4. **Terms of service contain:** the operator; what the service **is and is not** (e.g. \"a comparison\n   engine, not a seller\"; \"information, not medical/legal/financial advice\"); user obligations (lawful use,\n   age limit); limitation of liability; moderation and account termination; IP (users keep their content,\n   grant a license); **governing law of your jurisdiction**, complaints, how changes are announced, contact.\n5. **The privacy policy contains:** the controller; each data category with purpose, legal basis and\n   retention; third-party processors; user rights (access, erasure, export, objection); cookies; age; contact.\n6. **Consent before trackers.** Analytics and marketing scripts load only after consent.\n7. **Disclaimers fit the domain:** age gate for age-restricted content; \"this is not advice\" for\n   health/legal/finance; crisis resources per region for sensitive products (\u2192 [10](10-seo-and-translations.md)).\n8. **Terms \u2194 policy \u2194 UI \u2194 code say one thing.** A contradiction between layers is worse than a missing clause.\n9. **Legal documents go into Day 0 as placeholders**, not \"later\" (\u2192 [07](07-new-project-day-0.md)).\n\n## Security checklist (index)\n\nOne entry point; each rule is enforced where it lives.\n\n- **Secrets** in env / a secret store, rotated on leak (\u2192 [08](08-stack-and-technologies.md)).\n- **Auth and sessions:** bcrypt or OAuth, security headers, rate-limit; sessions in a persistent store (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n- **Authorization server-side:** *this* user may touch *this* row/action \u2014 not just \"logged in\" (IDOR).\n- **Input is hostile:** validate shape, parameterize SQL, treat external and user content as **data, not\n  instructions** (\u2192 [15](15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots.md)).\n- **Errors don't leak:** no stack traces, secrets or PII to users or logs (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n- **Quotas** on paid/expensive endpoints (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab An invented tax ID, address or company name in the product.\n- \ud83d\udeab Copy promising more than the product does (\"fully anonymous\", \"guaranteed prices\").\n- \ud83d\udeab Terms and policy naming different operators or retention periods.\n- \ud83d\udeab A legal page in one language inside a multilingual product.\n- \ud83d\udeab Analytics loaded before consent.\n", "10-seo-and-translations": "# 10 \u2014 SEO and translations\n\n> Commandment III applied to visibility: indexable, complete in every language, measured \u2014 not \"probably OK\".\n> Standard on-page SEO (unique title and description, canonical, sitemap, `robots.txt`, one `h1`, clean\n> slugs, OG cards) is assumed; these are the rules that are easy to get wrong.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Multilingual = reciprocal `hreflang` for every language pair + `x-default`.** Without it the search\n   engine serves the wrong language.\n2. **Language completeness is a failing test:** a missing key or page in any language breaks the build (\u2192 [03](03-testing-and-verification.md)).\n3. **Legal and UI pages exist in every language you ship** (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n4. **Programmatic pages need quality guards** (minimum items, ratings, sources) and a hand-written intro \u2014\n   a thin page generated at scale is spam.\n5. **YMYL topics (health, money, safety) demand E-E-A-T:** named author and operator, cited sources, no\n   regulated advice \u2014 a disclaimer and a crisis path instead.\n6. **The register is part of the translation:** correct but cold copy is a bug. Machine translation is fine\n   for the skeleton; **a human reviews disclaimers, crisis messages and legal copy.**\n7. **RTL languages mirror the whole layout**, not just the text.\n8. **Measure what converts** (Search Console, anonymized analytics) and let it pick the next page to write.\n9. **Core Web Vitals are a ranking signal** \u2192 measure and fix (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Keyword stuffing; thin programmatic pages.\n- \ud83d\udeab A multilingual site without `hreflang`.\n- \ud83d\udeab Unreviewed machine translation of sensitive copy.\n- \ud83d\udeab An English-only legal page in a multilingual product.\n", "11-data-model-and-normalization": "# 11 \u2014 Data model and normalization\n\n> Commandments V and VII at the root: the schema is the contract of the whole system. Normalize first;\n> denormalize deliberately, with a named source of truth.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Controlled vocabularies live in lookup tables** (countries, types, tags) plus a `CHECK` where values are\n   fixed \u2014 never free text in a column.\n2. **Many-to-many goes through a junction table**, never `primary_x` + `secondary_x` columns.\n3. **The stable identifier is the slug, not the numeric id.** Ids drift after merges; user data is mapped\n   `slug \u2192 new id`, a missing slug is skipped and logged (\u2192 [19](19-database-swap-runbook.md)).\n4. **Active row instead of overwrite where history matters:** expire the old row (`expired_at`), insert the\n   new one; a partial unique index (`WHERE expired_at IS NULL`) enforces one active row per key. Audit for free.\n5. **Computed values are computed, not stored** (e.g. a display name from its parts) \u2014 stored, they drift.\n6. **A cache or snapshot names its source of truth** and falls back to it when stale; never two sources for one fact.\n7. **Migrations are forward-only and additive**, scripts gate on a column/table existing, and every data\n   operation ends with `foreign_key_check` (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n8. **The schema is documented:** an ERD plus the allowed values of every controlled field, in `docs/`.\n9. **Declared privacy is enforced by the model:** sensitive processing runs as a separate idempotent\n   pipeline on **anonymized** data, with a test that re-identification is impossible (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Free text where a lookup belongs (\"USA\", \"U.S.A.\", \"United States\").\n- \ud83d\udeab One fact stored in two places without synchronization.\n- \ud83d\udeab User data mapped by a mutable id.\n- \ud83d\udeab A stored computed value that drifts.\n- \ud83d\udeab A destructive migration under running old code.\n", "12-flexibility-and-scalability": "# 12 \u2014 Flexibility and scalability\n\n> The golden rule of altitude applied to architecture: change without a rewrite, grow without a rebuild \u2014\n> and scale only when a metric demands it, never \"just in case\".\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Separate the layers by a contract, not by shared code:** data \u2194 ingest \u2194 web, bound by the schema\n   (\u2192 [11](11-data-model-and-normalization.md)) or an explicit API contract. Either side can then be replaced.\n2. **Changes are additive and backward-compatible:** a new column, endpoint or language doesn't break what works.\n3. **Feature flags arrive with the first risky change that reaches live users** \u2014 ship dark, then ramp.\n   Not on Day 0 of a prototype; never skipped once real users are at risk.\n4. **Keep the process stateless where you can**; sessions and state go to a store (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n5. **Platform is a recorded trade-off:** always-on VPS (fixed cost, no cold start) vs scale-to-zero (pay per\n   use, cold start) \u2014 chosen by traffic profile and budget, written down as an ADR.\n6. **Every cache has explicit invalidation** and a stated lifetime.\n7. **Pipelines are composable and idempotent:** each stage runs alone, a re-run is safe (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n8. **Global product = i18n from the first commit**, not bolted on (\u2192 [10](10-seo-and-translations.md)).\n9. **Scale by the numbers:** SQLite \u2192 PostgreSQL, monolith \u2192 services, only when latency, cost or size measurably\n   demand it \u2014 with an ADR (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Microservices, Kafka or Kubernetes for a hundred users.\n- \ud83d\udeab State in process memory that blocks restarts and scaling.\n- \ud83d\udeab A big-bang rewrite instead of additive steps.\n- \ud83d\udeab A risky change for live users with no flag and no ramp.\n- \ud83d\udeab Migrating to PostgreSQL \"just in case\" while SQLite hasn't broken a sweat.\n", "13-performance-frontend-and-sql": "# 13 \u2014 Performance: frontend and SQL\n\n> Commandment III applied to speed: \"feels faster\" doesn't exist \u2014 **before/after numbers** do. Standard\n> hygiene is assumed (indexes on hot `WHERE`/`JOIN` columns, no N+1, no `SELECT *`, WebP + `srcset`, lazy\n> loading below the fold, self-hosted fonts with `font-display: swap`, server-side pagination).\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Measure first, prove after.** Lighthouse / Core Web Vitals (LCP, CLS, INP) for the front,\n   `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` for SQL \u2014 reported **before and after**. No optimization without a number; an\n   unmeasured \"optimization\" can be a regression in disguise.\n2. **A Lighthouse baseline right after the first working page** \u2014 otherwise there's nothing to compare to.\n3. **Cache-bust per deploy:** statics are `immutable` with a long `max-age`, so every asset URL carries\n   `?v=<git-short-hash>` \u2014 otherwise users run the old app until a hard refresh (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n4. **Stream LLM responses** token by token (SSE) \u2014 never a blank screen until generation ends (\u2192 [15](15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots.md)).\n5. **Expensive aggregates are cached** (a stats snapshot with a live fallback), not recounted on every request.\n\n## Mobile: the viewport is a moving target\n\n6. **`100dvh`, not `100vh`, for full-screen sheets** \u2014 declare `100vh` first as a fallback, then `100dvh`\n   (the last rule an engine understands wins).\n7. **Keyboard-sticky elements anchor to `visualViewport`** (`height`/`offsetTop`, re-read on its\n   `resize`/`scroll`), not `position: fixed; bottom: 0`. Snap to the new size; don't animate the keyboard.\n8. **Reset `flex-basis` where the main axis flips** \u2014 a row's `flex: 1 1 220px` becomes a 220px-tall empty\n   box in a column (`flex: 0 0 auto` at that breakpoint).\n9. **The emulator lies:** measure real geometry (`getBoundingClientRect`) and keep a device-profile test\n   (e.g. a Playwright project pinned to a phone): no horizontal overflow, key element above the fold.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Optimizing without a measurement.\n- \ud83d\udeab Recomputing `COUNT(*)` over the whole database on every request.\n- \ud83d\udeab Blocking the UI until the full LLM response arrives.\n- \ud83d\udeab `100vh` sheets and `fixed; bottom: 0` inputs on mobile.\n- \ud83d\udeab \"Fixing\" mobile from a desktop-emulator screenshot.\n", "14-operational-resilience": "# 14 \u2014 Operational resilience and external dependencies\n\n> Commandments III, V, VI at runtime: prod lives in an unreliable world. The worst bugs don't shout \u2014 they\n> hang, log everyone out, or drain the budget. Almost every rule here was paid for by an incident.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **One bad request degrades that request, not the service:** process-level catchers for unhandled\n   rejections/exceptions, async routes that forward errors, a 500 handler that leaks no stack trace. Defend\n   against real edge data (an OAuth account without a password, `null` where a string is assumed).\n2. **Long jobs are resumable:** checkpoint completed units, save every N, idempotent end-to-end \u2014 a crash\n   costs the last batch, not the run.\n3. **Match the runner to the job's lifetime:** the agent's background survives turns but dies with the\n   session; anything that must outlive the conversation runs as a **detached process or OS-scheduled task**.\n4. **External sources are hostile:** a timeout on every call, retry with exponential backoff and a cap, and\n   **validate the response, not the status** \u2014 \"200 + an error page\" is a failure.\n5. **The provider's network breaks working code only in prod:** e.g. outbound SMTP 465 blocked \u2192 use 587 +\n   STARTTLS with hard connect/greeting timeouts. \"Sent\" \u2260 delivered: verified sender domain (SPF/DKIM) and a\n   real test send. Verify on the provider, not locally.\n6. **Every paid resource sits behind a hard quota** \u2014 per user and tier, enforced server-side, with a clear\n   message and the reset date shown up front.\n7. **Know prod is alive:** structured logs with levels (no secrets, no full PII), `/healthz`, an external\n   uptime ping, alerts on what hurts (5xx spikes, failed payments/mail, a job that didn't run), errors routed\n   somewhere you'll see them.\n8. **Recurring jobs: watch the result, not the runner.** Assert on the **age of the freshest record per\n   source** (`MAX(updated_at)` grouped by source), not on an exit code \u2014 a green report once ran for three\n   days beside 44% of the data frozen. A report covers only the paths it knows about.\n9. **Scheduler settings are production and live outside the repo** (retry after a missed start, power and\n   network conditions, account, working directory) \u2014 write them into the runbook and re-check after any OS,\n   hardware or account change.\n10. **Every incident becomes a dated, numbered lesson in the runbook** (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab No global error catcher \u2014 one bad request restarts the service for everyone.\n- \ud83d\udeab A long scraper without checkpoints, run from the session background.\n- \ud83d\udeab An external call without timeout and backoff.\n- \ud83d\udeab A paid API without a quota.\n- \ud83d\udeab Sessions in process memory \u2014 every deploy logs everyone out.\n- \ud83d\udeab A recurring job watched by its own exit code.\n- \ud83d\udeab Secrets or PII in logs.\n", "15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots": "# 15 \u2014 Scraping, AI APIs and configurable chatbots\n\n> Web data and AI as product features \u2014 effective, cheap, and **under a contract**. The operational layer\n> (timeouts, backoff, quotas, resumability) \u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md).\n\n## Scraping\n\n1. **An official source first** (API, feed, export, sitemap); HTML scraping is the last resort.\n2. **Respect the source:** `robots.txt`/ToS, a reasonable throttle, a truthful User-Agent (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n3. **A site answers twice \u2014 policy and machinery.** When `robots.txt` allows the read but a bot shield\n   blocks it, identify yourself and **ask the owner**; never engineer a way around the shield. If the request\n   fails, the source leaves the product.\n4. **Tell an IP block from a shield before building anything:** 403 here but 200 from another network = the\n   address is blocked (relocate the fetch); 403 from every network = the shield is the point.\n5. **Validate the shape of the result**, not the status \u2014 selector drift raises an alert, never silent zeros.\n6. **Normalize at the entry point** against lookup tables (\u2192 [11](11-data-model-and-normalization.md)); fetch\n   the **delta**, not the whole catalog; loaders are idempotent (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n\n## AI APIs\n\n7. **An LLM only where deterministic code can't cope** (unstructured extraction, classification, summaries) \u2014\n   never for what a regex, SQL or a function does.\n8. **Force an output contract:** JSON schema / tool use, validation, retry on mismatch. LLM output is untrusted input.\n9. **Cache identical inputs; quota every paid call; enrich offline in a checkpointed batch** rather than live in\n   a user request. Measure cost and latency per task.\n\n## Chatbots\n\n10. **Persona, scope and rules are configuration** (versioned data), not code \u2014 tuned without a deploy.\n11. **Grounded in your data, with the source cited**; a safe \"I don't know\" instead of invention.\n12. **User and web content are data, never instructions** (prompt injection): no side-effect action without\n    confirmation, no leaking the system prompt or secrets.\n13. **A golden set of questions runs like tests** on every prompt change.\n14. **A disclaimer and a privacy basis for stored conversations** (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab Scraping when an API exists; a full re-scrape every night.\n- \ud83d\udeab Evading a bot shield the owner put up.\n- \ud83d\udeab Parsing free prose and hoping for the format.\n- \ud83d\udeab No cache and no quota on paid calls.\n- \ud83d\udeab A persona baked into the code; an ungrounded assistant.\n- \ud83d\udeab Acting on a chat message without confirmation.\n", "16-driving-claude": "# 16 \u2014 Driving Claude\n\n> The agent's features are documented by the agent itself; this chapter only sets **project policy** for\n> using them \u2014 when they are required, and where the limits are.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Plan mode is required** when the shape of the work is open, the blast radius is wide (migration,\n   tree-wide rename, URLs/redirects) or the change is hard to undo. The plan names the files, the trade-off\n   and **how the result will be verified**. Skip it for a bounded fix.\n2. **Autopilot is the default \u2014 inside the permission map** (\u2192 [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md)). The\n   middle and right columns still stop it; that is what makes autopilot safe.\n3. **Model by stakes:** a cheaper model for mechanical work, the strongest for architecture, hard bugs and\n   anything that can lose data. Never economize on a data-losing operation.\n4. **Long work in the background; anything that must outlive the session \u2014 detached** (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n5. **Fan out to subagents** when breadth, parallel independent edits or adversarial verification genuinely\n   help \u2014 keep the conclusion, not the dumps. Not for a one-line fix.\n6. **One concurrent session = one `git worktree`.** Two sessions in one checkout share an index: they stage\n   each other's files and sweep half-done edits into commits (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n7. **Repeated procedures become skills; \"always do X after Y\" becomes a hook** (\u2192 [02](02-skills-and-refactoring.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab A wide or irreversible change without plan mode \u2014 you review the diff instead of the decision.\n- \ud83d\udeab Hand-confirming every safe edit \u2014 slow, and it trains rubber-stamping.\n- \ud83d\udeab A cheap model on a migration that can lose data.\n- \ud83d\udeab Two sessions in one checkout.\n- \ud83d\udeab A job that must outlive the session left in the session background.\n", "17-language-and-guards": "# 17 \u2014 Language of code, and guards that keep rules alive\n\n> Commandments I and III applied to names: a rule that lives only in someone's head is not a rule, and a\n> rule without a test is a wish.\n\n## Two languages, split by reader\n\n| Layer | Language |\n|---|---|\n| **Code** \u2014 identifiers (modules, classes, functions, variables, fixtures, **test names**), code file names, DB tables and columns, CLI flags, config and API keys, enum/state values, log keys, commit messages | **English, always** |\n| **Prose** \u2014 docs, plans, `AI_README`, comments, docstrings, user-facing text | **the project's language**, declared once in `CLAUDE.md` |\n\n```\n# CLAUDE.md\nCode: English (identifiers, tests, DB, CLI, keys). Prose: Polish (docs, comments, docstrings, UI).\n```\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **English code is the ecosystem's language** \u2014 tooling, autocomplete, errors and the next engineer all\n   speak it; a native-language identifier is transliterated three different ways.\n2. **Tests are code.** `test_<subject>_<condition>_<expected>` in English; the *why* (the incident, the date,\n   the cost) goes into the docstring, in the prose language.\n3. **No mixed identifiers** (`test_cost_NIE_ISTNIEJE_in_window`) \u2014 unreadable and ungreppable.\n4. **Names a tool prints stay English inside prose** \u2014 a command, flag, status, file or tag (`commit`,\n   `--dry-run`, `migrate.py`, `deploy-*`): that is what you grep for. The everyday word for the activity may\n   be in the prose language (\"wdro\u017cenie\" for deploying).\n5. **A rule without a guard is a wish.** One session shipped 95 of 98 commits without touching the index\n   it was told to update. Rules that must survive a long session are enforced by the test suite.\n\n## The two guards (ready-made in `guards/`)\n\nCopy them into the project's `tests/` as `test_ai_readme.py` and `test_code_language.py`, adjust the\ndirectory lists, run them, commit the baseline \u2014 in the **first commit** (\u2192 [07](07-new-project-day-0.md)).\nThey are **pytest reference implementations**: a Python project copies them; a Go, Rust, Kotlin or TypeScript\nproject ports the same spec to its own test runner (the index guard needs only file globs; the language guard\nneeds that language's identifier parser or linter). The rule is the spec, not the Python file.\n\n- **[`guards/ai_readme_guard.py`](guards/ai_readme_guard.py)** \u2014 every directory with code has an\n  `AI_README.md`; **every module is named in it**; a **20 kB ceiling** (past it the index stops being an index \u2014\n  split the directory, not the file); the list of guarded directories guards itself. It cannot tell whether a\n  description is *current* \u2014 that stays the writer's duty.\n- **[`guards/code_language_guard.py`](guards/code_language_guard.py)** \u2014 flags identifiers that look like the\n  prose language (stop-list of function words + letter shape); a **ratchet** with a per-file baseline.\n\n## The ratchet \u2014 introducing any rule into an old codebase\n\n1. **Freeze** today's count per file in a committed baseline (empty on a new project).\n2. **New files must be clean** from the day the rule is written.\n3. **Counts may only fall;** when one falls, lower the baseline **on command** (`--write-baseline`), never silently.\n4. **Raising a baseline by hand is weakening the guard** \u2014 the same sin as skipping a test.\n5. **Exceptions are dated**, capped at today's size, and tied to the backlog item that removes them.\n\nVendoring into an existing project: run the code-language guard once, commit the baseline as it is, and put\n\"rename foreign identifiers \u2014 N left\" into the backlog with the number.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab \"Test names don't count as code.\"\n- \ud83d\udeab Mixed-language identifiers.\n- \ud83d\udeab Translating a name the tool prints (`--dry-run` written as \"pr\u00f3ba\").\n- \ud83d\udeab A rule in `CLAUDE.md` with no test behind it.\n- \ud83d\udeab Raising the baseline to make the suite green.\n- \ud83d\udeab An `AI_README` past the ceiling \"because everything here matters\".\n", "18-release-rehearsal": "# 18 \u2014 Rehearse the release: dev \u2192 preprod \u2192 prod\n\n> Commandments V and VI: a migration or a database swap run for the **first time** in the prod\n> maintenance window is tested on your users. Run the exact runbook once on a faithful copy first.\n\n## Three environments, three jobs\n\n| Environment | Job | Data |\n|---|---|---|\n| **dev** (your machine) | build, reproduce bugs | seed data, or an **anonymised** refresh from a prod backup |\n| **preprod** (server clone of prod: same OS, web server, process manager, TLS) | rehearse migrations and the deploy itself | anonymised prod backup; **basic-auth gated, `noindex`** |\n| **prod** | serve users | sacred \u2014 touched only on \"deploy\", only with steps rehearsed on preprod the same day |\n\nAdd preprod when the project reaches the third branching rung or the first risky migration (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Build preprod from prod the same way every time \u2014 scripted:** clone with prod's deploy key; refresh the\n   database from a prod backup; **anonymise PII before it leaves prod** (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n2. **Environment-specific values are env-overridable, never hard-coded:** `SITE_URL`, canonical, OG, sitemap\n   point at preprod \u2014 otherwise preprod leaks prod URLs or gets indexed (\u2192 [10](10-seo-and-translations.md)).\n3. **Rehearse the real runbook, not a simplified one:** run the migration, time the window, smoke-test,\n   confirm it is forward-only and idempotent (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n4. **Expect the gotchas only a real preprod surfaces:** the ACME/Let's Encrypt `http-01` challenge must\n   bypass basic-auth or renewal fails with 401; a private repo needs its SSH remote before the first pull.\n5. **Preprod is a rehearsal stage, never a second prod:** no live users, a stale DB is fine, and **its\n   database is never swapped back into prod** (\u2192 [11](11-data-model-and-normalization.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab A migration whose first run is the prod maintenance window.\n- \ud83d\udeab Real, non-anonymised user data on preprod \u2014 a second copy of your PII liability on a weaker box.\n- \ud83d\udeab Preprod indexed or emitting prod URLs.\n- \ud83d\udeab Treating preprod's data as a source of truth.\n", "19-database-swap-runbook": "# 19 \u2014 Database swap runbook\n\n> Commandment VII at its hardest: replace the prod database wholesale (e.g. a rebuilt catalog) **while\n> keeping every live account and everything users created.** Rare \u2014 and where most traps are.\n\n## Rules that save user data\n\n1. **\"Users\" is many tables.** Enumerate **every** table with a foreign key to the user (accounts, carts,\n   reviews, likes, badges, sessions, chat sessions + messages + limits, feedback\u2026). A table without\n   `user_id` is mapped through its parent. Gate each on the table/column existing.\n2. **Map by a stable key (slug \u2192 new id), never by the old id.** A missing slug is skipped and logged.\n3. **The source of truth for accounts is live prod**, read after `stop` inside the maintenance window \u2014 not an\n   older snapshot that loses the last hour's registrations.\n4. **FK-safe order:** delete children before parents, insert parents before children.\n5. **Back up live prod right before the swap** (`.backup replaced-prod-<ts>.db`) \u2014 that is the rollback.\n6. **Verify the merged database:** account count = prod, 0 dangling references, `integrity_check: ok`, FK\n   violations not above the pre-existing level.\n7. **Sessions live in their own store** (e.g. `sessions.db`), never in the swapped database and never in\n   process memory \u2014 otherwise the swap or any restart logs everyone out (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n\n## Sequence\n\n1. **Live phase:** push the code; copy git-ignored assets **before** the window (rsync / tar over ssh, only\n   the delta) \u2014 they don't ride with `git pull`; upload the new snapshot to the server.\n2. **Window (seconds, atomic, `set -e`):** flag ON \u2192 pull \u2192 stop \u2192 back up live prod \u2192 migrate users\n   (source = prod) \u2192 swap the file (remove `-wal`/`-shm`, move, re-enable WAL) \u2192 start \u2192 **smoke** \u2192 flag OFF.\n3. **Smoke via `localhost`** (bypasses the maintenance page): key pages 200, redirects, row counts, process\n   logs clean. A trivial, safe bug found in the window is fixed in the window \u2014 don't release a known 500.\n4. **After:** tag the deploy, clean temp files, write the lessons into the runbook (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- \ud83d\udeab A swap \"except accounts\" that only keeps the `users` table \u2014 reviews, badges and chat are lost.\n- \ud83d\udeab Mapping user data by id \u2014 reviews land on the wrong product.\n- \ud83d\udeab Migrating accounts from an old snapshot.\n- \ud83d\udeab Moving gigabytes of assets inside the window.\n- \ud83d\udeab `scp` of a live `.db` \u2014 use `.backup`; WAL holds the fresh pages.\n", "README": "# The Craft \u2014 Rzemios\u0142o <sub>(Kolada Build)</sub>\n\n> **Hard rules for building and running an application end-to-end with Claude** \u2014 a terse doctrine the\n> agent reads and follows, so a vibe-coded project stays understandable, testable, safe to change and\n> trustworthy, from the first commit to production with real users.\n\nStart with **[What The Craft is](intro.md)** or the whole doctrine on one screen: **[00 \u2014 Decalogue](00-commandments.md)**.\nExplanations, examples and stories live on the website: **[thecraft.jakub.solutions](https://thecraft.jakub.solutions)**.\n\n## Adopt it in your project\n\nThe Craft is meant to be dropped into a project as `docs/rules/`, so the agent reads it from Day 0:\n\n```bash\ngit submodule add https://github.com/JKolada/TheCraftWithClaude docs/rules\n# or just copy the files into docs/rules/\n```\n\nThen put a plain pointer at the top of your project's `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` (full steps \u2192\n[07 \u2014 New project: Day 0](07-new-project-day-0.md)):\n\n> This project follows the build rules in `docs/rules/` (The Craft); they override default behavior.\n> Read `docs/rules/00-commandments.md` first, then `grep -i <keyword> docs/rules/AI_README.md` and read\n> only the matched chapter.\n\nThe ready-made test guards from chapter 17 are in [`guards/`](guards/).\n\n## Languages\n\n**English is the base** \u2014 the canonical doctrine lives in the repo root. **Polski** is a 1:1 translation\nunder [`pl/`](pl/00-commandments.md) (same chapters, same filenames). The in-browser reader\n([index.html](index.html)) renders the `.md` and has an **EN | PL** switch (default EN). On GitHub the\n`.md` files render natively \u2014 just click the links below.\n\n## Chapters\n\n| # | Chapter | What it covers |\n|---|---------|----------------|\n| 00 | [Decalogue](00-commandments.md) | 10 commandments \u2014 the doctrine's core, to memorize |\n| 01 | [Documentation and AI_README](01-documentation-and-ai-readme.md) | `AI_README` in every directory, `CLAUDE.md` as source of truth, the grep index, docs as code |\n| 02 | [Skills and refactoring](02-skills-and-refactoring.md) | When to build a skill/slash-command, refactoring discipline, SOLID |\n| 03 | [Testing and verification](03-testing-and-verification.md) | Every behavior change carries a test, \"verify, don't declare\", guards proven by the incident |\n| 04 | [Scripts and databases](04-scripts-and-databases.md) | Idempotency, dry-run/--execute, forward-only migrations, backups |\n| 05 | [Git and deployments](05-git-and-deployments.md) | Search history, undo, parallel sessions and branching, the deploy policy and deploy tags |\n| 06 | [Collaboration and memory](06-collaboration-and-memory.md) | Plan\u2192sketch\u2192scale, the permission map (free/ask/never), change summary, handoff when context runs out |\n| 07 | [New project: Day 0](07-new-project-day-0.md) | Wire The Craft in + product brief + a concrete startup checklist |\n| 08 | [Stack and technologies](08-stack-and-technologies.md) | Defaults: Python, SQLite, server-rendered HTML, one VPS monolith \u2014 and when to depart |\n| 09 | [Law and protecting the creator](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md) | Terms, privacy policy, disclaimers as the creator's armor |\n| 10 | [SEO and translations](10-seo-and-translations.md) | hreflang, JSON-LD, programmatic SEO, E-E-A-T/YMYL, language parity as a test |\n| 11 | [Data model and normalization](11-data-model-and-normalization.md) | Lookup tables, slug instead of ID, active-row, deliberate denormalization |\n| 12 | [Flexibility and scalability](12-flexibility-and-scalability.md) | Separate the layers, feature flags, scale-to-zero vs always-on, don't over-engineer |\n| 13 | [Performance: frontend and SQL](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md) | Measure before/after, cache-busting per deploy, streaming, mobile viewport (dvh/visualViewport) |\n| 14 | [Operational resilience](14-operational-resilience.md) | Crash-proof runtime, resumable jobs, hostile external sources, provider limits, quotas, recurring-job watchdogs |\n| 15 | [Scraping, AI APIs, and chatbots](15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots.md) | Effective scraping, AI for specific tasks (contract + cache), configurable & grounded assistants |\n| 16 | [Driving Claude](16-driving-claude.md) | Project policy for plan mode, autopilot, model choice, background work, subagents, worktrees |\n| 17 | [Language of code and guards](17-language-and-guards.md) | Code in English (identifiers, tests, DB, CLI), prose in the project's language; the `AI_README` index guard and the code-language ratchet |\n| 18 | [Rehearse the release](18-release-rehearsal.md) | dev \u2192 preprod \u2192 prod: rehearse migrations and the deploy on a faithful, anonymised copy of prod. |\n| 19 | [Database swap runbook](19-database-swap-runbook.md) | Swap a prod database while keeping live accounts: every FK table, slug not ID, live prod as source, maintenance window, smoke. |\n\n## The philosophy in one sentence\n\n> **Build like a craftsman \u2014 slow where a mistake is expensive, fast where it's cheap; and leave behind a\n> workshop where the next person (human or agent) immediately knows where everything is.**\n\n---\n\n*A living document \u2014 updated after every project that taught us something. A doctrine that doesn't change\nis dead.* Version 1.1.1 was reviewed and tightened with **Claude Opus 5.5**. \u00b7 Part of **[jakub.solutions](https://jakub.solutions)** \u2014 Jakub Kolada.\nMaintainer/build docs: [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md) \u00b7 [AI_README.md](AI_README.md).\n", "intro": "# What The Craft is\n\n> **A set of hard rules for building and running an application end-to-end with Claude** \u2014 so a\n> vibe-coded project stays understandable, testable, safe to change and trustworthy, from the first commit\n> to production with real users. Not a framework, not a library: a doctrine the agent reads and follows.\n\n## Who it's for\n\nA **single developer** building a real application **with AI as the pair** \u2014 not an enterprise team with\ndedicated ops and QA. \"Solo\" still means several writers: you'll run **several Claude sessions** on one repo,\nso the coordination rules (issues, short branches, push/pull discipline) matter from the second session on\n(\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n\n## How to start\n\n1. Install **Claude Code** (desktop or CLI).\n2. Fill in the [brief](index.html#brief) \u2014 it asks what changes the architecture: languages, MVP,\n   monetization, tone.\n3. Attach The Craft to the project as `docs/rules/` and start from [Day 0](07-new-project-day-0.md).\n\nYou don't have to memorize anything \u2014 the agent reads the rules. If you read one file, read the\n[Decalogue](00-commandments.md).\n\nThese pages are deliberately terse. **Explanations, examples and the stories behind each rule:**\n[thecraft.jakub.solutions](https://thecraft.jakub.solutions).\n"}, "pl": {"00-commandments": "# 00 \u2014 Dekalog\n\nRdze\u0144. Je\u015bli czytasz jeden plik z tego zestawu \u2014 czytaj ten. Ka\u017cde przykazanie rozwija dalszy rozdzia\u0142.\nTo s\u0105 **regu\u0142y, kt\u00f3rych projekt musi przestrzega\u0107**, celowo zwi\u0119z\u0142e \u2014 wyja\u015bnienia, przyk\u0142ady i historie s\u0105\nna stronie: [thecraft.jakub.solutions](https://thecraft.jakub.solutions).\n\n> **Zasada zerowa (aktywacja).** Widzisz w projekcie pakiet `docs/rules/` (The Craft)? Traktuj go jako\n> nadrz\u0119dn\u0105 doktryn\u0119 prowadzenia tego projektu. Przeczytaj **ten plik**, potem `grep -i <temat>` po\n> `AI_README.md` i przeczytaj **tylko** trafiony rozdzia\u0142 \u2014 nigdy ca\u0142ej paczki co sesj\u0119 (to pali kontekst,\n> kt\u00f3ry mia\u0142e\u015b oszcz\u0119dzi\u0107 \u2192 [01](01-documentation-and-ai-readme.md)). Utrwal to: kr\u00f3tki wska\u017anik zwyk\u0142ym\n> j\u0119zykiem na g\u00f3rze `CLAUDE.md` (\u0142adowany co sesj\u0119) i `AGENTS.md` (inni agenci) \u2014 **wska\u017anik, nie wklejka**,\n> i bez udawanych etykiet `SYSTEM`/`URGENT`: deklaracja autorytetu w pliku wygl\u0105da dla wsp\u00f3\u0142czesnego modelu\n> jak prompt injection. Niewpi\u0119ty pakiet nigdy nie jest czytany. Kopia wendorowana **nie jest** konstytucj\u0105\n> gospodarza \u2014 jego w\u0142asny `CLAUDE.md` ma pierwsze\u0144stwo. Kroki \u2192 [07](07-new-project-day-0.md).\n> (Dekalog zostaje dziesi\u0105tk\u0105; to aktywacja, nie jedenasta regu\u0142a.)\n\n---\n\n### I. Dokumentuj dla agenta, nie dla archiwum.\nKa\u017cdy katalog z kodem ma `AI_README.md`, aktualizowany **przed** commitem, i test, kt\u00f3ry pada, gdy nie jest\n(\u2192 [17](17-language-and-guards.md)). Dokumentacja pisana \u201ena ko\u0144cu\" rozje\u017cd\u017ca si\u0119 i k\u0142amie. \u2192 [01](01-documentation-and-ai-readme.md)\n\n### II. Szukaj w historii, zanim napiszesz linijk\u0119.\n`git log -S`, `git log --grep`, `git blame`, `AI_README` katalogu. Wi\u0119kszo\u015b\u0107 \u201enowych\" problem\u00f3w ju\u017c tu\nrozwi\u0105zano \u2014 jest helper, by\u0142a migracja, dziwny kszta\u0142t ma pow\u00f3d. \u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)\n\n### III. Weryfikuj, nie deklaruj.\n\u201eDzia\u0142a\" znaczy **dow\u00f3d**: przebieg test\u00f3w, kod HTTP, liczby, screenshot. Pora\u017cki raportujesz z outputem;\npomini\u0119te kroki \u2014 jako pomini\u0119te. \u2192 [03](03-testing-and-verification.md)\n\n### IV. Dry-run jest domy\u015blny; `--execute` jest \u015bwiadomy.\nKa\u017cdy skrypt zmieniaj\u0105cy dane najpierw **wypisuje plan** (co, ile, gdzie). Mutacja rusza dopiero po\nprzeczytaniu planu. \u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)\n\n### V. Backup to mechanizm rollbacku, nie ostro\u017cno\u015b\u0107.\nMigracje **forward-only**. Snapshot **przed** ka\u017cd\u0105 zmian\u0105 schematu lub danych na prodzie; cofni\u0119cie schematu\n= przywr\u00f3cenie tego snapshotu. \u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)\n\n### VI. Prod jest \u015bwi\u0119ty \u2014 nic go nie dotyka bez wyra\u017anego \u201ewdra\u017caj\".\nCommit i push na \u017cyczenie to nie deploy. Pull na serwerze, reload, migracja, swap bazy, flaga maintenance \u2014\ntylko na wyra\u017ane s\u0142owo u\u017cytkownika. Dzia\u0142ania nieodwracalne i \u201ena zewn\u0105trz\" zawsze potwierdzasz; zgoda nie\nprzechodzi na nast\u0119pn\u0105 akcj\u0119. \u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md), [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md)\n\n### VII. Dane u\u017cytkownik\u00f3w s\u0105 nienaruszalne.\nNigdy ich nie gub, nie wyciekaj, nie mutuj bez backupu. Odwo\u0142uj si\u0119 do nich **stabilnym kluczem** (slug),\nnigdy dryfuj\u0105cym ID; brak dopasowania pomijasz i logujesz, nie wciskasz na si\u0142\u0119. Po ka\u017cdej operacji na nich\npolicz wiersze i odpal sprawdzenie integralno\u015bci. Pe\u0142ny swap bazy proda \u2192 [19](19-database-swap-runbook.md).\n\n### VIII. Taguj ka\u017cdy deploy i m\u00f3w userowi, co zyska\u0142.\nAnnotated tag (`deploy-RRRR-MM-DD`) to jedyny stabilny znacznik \u201eco jest live\" i podstawa rollbacku. Ka\u017cdy\ndeploy aktualizuje publiczny changelog prostym j\u0119zykiem \u2014 co user zyskuje, bez \u017cargonu. \u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)\n\n### IX. Ma\u0142y, sp\u00f3jny commit; jeden temat.\nNiezwi\u0105zane zmiany id\u0105 do osobnych commit\u00f3w. Trzymaj `git status` czysty: \u015bmieci (`.bak`, pliki tymczasowe,\nprzypadkowe bazy) i sieroty (cudza niezacommitowana praca) wy\u0142apuj **wcze\u015bnie**. \u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)\n\n### X. Plan \u2192 iteruj \u2192 review.\nNajpierw plan albo szkic (3 przyk\u0142ady, nie 100), feedback, potem skala. UX dowo\u017a z **ciep\u0142em**, nie tylko\npoprawnie \u2014 produkt ma by\u0107 przyjemny, nie tylko dzia\u0142aj\u0105cy. \u2192 [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md)\n\n---\n\n## Siedem grzech\u00f3w g\u0142\u00f3wnych\n\n1. **Nad\u0119ty optymizm** \u2014 \u201ewszystko dzia\u0142a\" bez dowodu. (III)\n2. **Mutacja bez planu** \u2014 skrypt zmieniaj\u0105cy dane odpalony od razu z `--execute`. (IV)\n3. **Auto-deploy** \u2014 \u201egotowe, wi\u0119c idzie na prod\". (VI)\n4. **Dane user\u00f3w na wyczucie** \u2014 mapowanie po ID albo swap bez wyliczenia ka\u017cdej tabeli wskazuj\u0105cej na usera. (VII)\n5. **Commit-\u015bmietnik** \u2014 niezwi\u0105zane zmiany i artefakty w jednym commicie. (IX)\n6. **Skalowanie przed review** \u2014 500 sztuk, zanim user zobaczy\u0142 3. (X)\n7. **Dokumentacja \u201ep\u00f3\u017aniej\"** \u2014 kod idzie, `AI_README` zostaje w tyle, nast\u0119pna sesja b\u0142\u0105dzi. (I)\n\n---\n\n## Z\u0142ota zasada altytudy\n\n> **Wolno tam, gdzie b\u0142\u0105d jest drogi (prod, dane user\u00f3w, schemat). Szybko tam, gdzie jest tani (lokalny\n> eksperyment, szkic UI, dry-run).** Tempo dobieraj do kosztu pomy\u0142ki, nie do niecierpliwo\u015bci.\n\n---\n\n## Odst\u0119pstwo od regu\u0142y \u2014 i pod\u0142oga, kt\u00f3ra si\u0119 nie rusza\n\nTo default, nie klatka. Doktryna, kt\u00f3ra nie umie si\u0119 ugi\u0105\u0107, zostaje zignorowana w ca\u0142o\u015bci.\n\n- **Odst\u0119puj na g\u0142os:** nazwij regu\u0142\u0119, powiedz, czemu *tutaj* nie pasuje, zaproponuj zamiennik, uzyskaj\n  \u201etak\", zapisz decyzj\u0119 (ADR / `CLAUDE.md`). **Ciche pomini\u0119cie regu\u0142y to grzech, nie odst\u0119pstwo.**\n- **Precedencja:** root `CLAUDE.md` gospodarza > ta doktryna (`docs/rules/`) > defaulty agenta.\n- **Regu\u0142a, kt\u00f3r\u0105 wci\u0105\u017c nadpisujesz, to bug doktryny** \u2014 napraw j\u0105 u \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142a (w kanonie, z t\u0142umaczeniem),\n  zamiast omija\u0107 co sesj\u0119.\n\n> **Pod\u0142oga \u2014 bez negocjacji:** uczciwy raport (**III**), backup przed zmian\u0105 schematu/danych (**V**),\n> wyra\u017ane \u201ewdra\u017caj\" przed prodem (**VI**), dane u\u017cytkownik\u00f3w (**VII**). Pe\u0142na granica jako jedna tabela \u2192 [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md).\n", "01-documentation-and-ai-readme": "# 01 \u2014 Dokumentacja i AI_README\n\n> Przykazanie I: *dokumentuj dla agenta, nie dla archiwum.* Cel: nast\u0119pna sesja rozumie katalog bez\n> czytania ca\u0142ego jego kodu.\n\n## Trzy warstwy, podzielone wg czytelnika\n\n| Warstwa | Plik | Zawiera |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Konstytucja** | `CLAUDE.md` (root) | czym jest projekt, dok\u0142adne komendy uruchomienia, **polityki nadpisuj\u0105ce domy\u015blne zachowanie**, datowany \u201eStan bie\u017c\u0105cy\" z liczbami. \u0141adowany co sesj\u0119. |\n| **Mapa katalogu** | `AI_README.md` w ka\u017cdym katalogu z kodem | indeks modu\u0142\u00f3w, kluczowe API (sygnatura + kontrakt), pu\u0142apki, liczby, indeks grep. Czytany **zanim** dotkniesz katalogu. |\n| **Referencja / plany** | `docs/` | architektura, model danych, runbooki, `plans/`. |\n\n`README.md` to publiczne drzwi wej\u015bciowe (czym jest, jak zacz\u0105\u0107) \u2014 nie warstwa dla agenta. **Gdzie wpisa\u0107\nfakt? Zapytaj, kto go czyta** \u2014 i wpisz raz; pozosta\u0142e pliki linkuj\u0105.\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **`CLAUDE.md` wskazuje na mapy `AI_README.md`** \u2014 bez tego wska\u017anika nowa sesja nie wie, \u017ce istniej\u0105.\n2. **`AI_README.md` aktualizujesz przed commitem**, w tym samym commicie co kod. Indeksu pilnuje test\n   (ka\u017cdy modu\u0142 wymieniony, sufit rozmiaru) \u2192 [17](17-language-and-guards.md).\n3. **Indeks pisz pod `grep`, nie do czytania.** G\u0119ste od s\u0142\u00f3w kluczowych jednolinijkowce: konkretne nazwy,\n   synonimy, oba j\u0119zyki w projekcie dwuj\u0119zycznym. `grep -i <temat>` ma trafi\u0107 **jedn\u0105 lini\u0119** wskazuj\u0105c\u0105 **jeden plik**.\n4. **Jeden plik = jeden temat.** Temat rozsypany po pi\u0119ciu plikach to pi\u0119\u0107 trafie\u0144 i spalony kontekst.\n5. **Stan, nie intencja.** Opisuj, co kod robi teraz; kasuj wpisy usuni\u0119tego kodu \u2014 martwy wpis jest gorszy\n   ni\u017c \u017caden. Liczby (rekordy, testy) si\u0119 starzej\u0105 \u2192 aktualizuj je.\n6. **`docs/` opisuje wy\u0142\u0105cznie bie\u017c\u0105c\u0105 wersj\u0119.** Historia \u017cyje w gicie i `CHANGELOG`; aktualizujesz dokument,\n   nie trzymasz starej wersji obok.\n7. **`docs/plans/` to \u017cywy backlog.** Problem \u2192 opcje \u2192 wyb\u00f3r \u2192 dlaczego. Gdy plan wejdzie, wpisz decyzj\u0119 do\n   dokumentu / `CLAUDE.md`, kt\u00f3rym rz\u0105dzi, i **usu\u0144 plan** \u2014 git trzyma zapis.\n8. **Nowy katalog powstaje razem ze swoim `AI_README.md`** \u2014 nigdy \u201ep\u00f3\u017aniej\".\n9. **Artefakty generowane (HTML, snapshoty) to pochodne:** commituj, nigdy nie edytuj r\u0119cznie, regeneruj na\n   polecenie (skill), nie przy ka\u017cdym commicie.\n\nAktualizuj docsy przy: nowym module/funkcji/skrypcie/fladze, zmianie schematu, zmianie zachowania (pr\u00f3g,\nfallback), zmianie liczb. Nie przy: czystym bugfixie bez zmiany API, liter\u00f3wkach.\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab \u201eUzupe\u0142ni\u0119 docsy na ko\u0144cu\" \u2014 koniec nie nadchodzi, docsy k\u0142ami\u0105.\n- \ud83d\udeab `AI_README` opisuj\u0105cy \u017cyczenia zamiast stanu.\n- \ud83d\udeab Ten sam stan skopiowany do pi\u0119ciu plik\u00f3w \u2014 jedno \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142o prawdy, reszta linkuje.\n- \ud83d\udeab Og\u00f3lnikowe opisy (\u201ehelpery\", \u201er\u00f3\u017cne regu\u0142y\") \u2014 grep pud\u0142uje, agent czyta wszystko.\n", "02-skills-and-refactoring": "# 02 \u2014 Skille i refaktoring\n\n> Przykazania I i X: automatyzuj to, co si\u0119 powtarza; refaktoruj tak, by kod czyta\u0142 si\u0119 jak s\u0105siedni.\n\n## Skille (slash-komendy)\n\n1. **Zbuduj skill, gdy** procedura si\u0119 powtarza (\u22653 razy), \u0142atwo pomyli\u0107 kolejno\u015b\u0107 krok\u00f3w (migracja:\n   numer \u2192 SQL \u2192 zastosuj \u2192 `AI_README` \u2192 commit) albo niesie pu\u0142apk\u0119 specyficzn\u0105 dla projektu\n   (`PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8` na Windows, `--runInBand` przy dzia\u0142aj\u0105cym serwerze).\n2. **Skill ma kontrakt wyj\u015bcia** (exit 0 = zielono, inaczej: co pad\u0142o) i **wypisuje wszystko, co niszczy**.\n3. **Skill to przepis dla agenta; hook to automat odpalany przez harness.** \u201eZawsze r\u00f3b X po Y\" = hook\n   w configu, nie skill i nie pami\u0119\u0107.\n4. Zestaw startowy: `/run` (serwer + smoke), `/run-tests`, `/update-ai-readme`, `/add-migration` (baza relacyjna).\n\n## Refaktoring\n\n1. **Najpierw szukaj prior art** (`git log -S`, `git blame`) \u2014 \u201ebrzydki\" kszta\u0142t cz\u0119sto ma pow\u00f3d.\n2. **Refaktor osobno od zmiany zachowania** \u2014 jeden commit to albo porz\u0105dki, albo funkcja.\n3. **Reuse przed przepisaniem** \u2014 sprawd\u017a, czy helper ju\u017c istnieje.\n4. **Stare punkty wej\u015bcia dzia\u0142aj\u0105** przy przenosinach (re-eksportuj\u0105ce shimy) albo aktualizujesz wszystkich\n   wywo\u0142uj\u0105cych w tej samej zmianie.\n5. **Ma\u0142e, sprawdzone kroki:** refaktor \u2192 testy zielone \u2192 commit.\n6. **SOLID proporcjonalnie:** jeden pow\u00f3d do zmiany na modu\u0142; rozszerzaj za flag\u0105 zamiast edytowa\u0107 gor\u0105c\u0105\n   \u015bcie\u017ck\u0119 (\u2192 [12](12-flexibility-and-scalability.md)); **\u017cadnej abstrakcji przed drugim konkretnym przypadkiem.**\n7. **Dwa tryby review, nigdy razem:** upraszczanie (reuse, czytelno\u015b\u0107) vs poprawno\u015b\u0107 (adwersarialne szukanie bug\u00f3w).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Skill na co\u015b robionego raz.\n- \ud83d\udeab Skill, kt\u00f3ry po cichu robi co\u015b destrukcyjnego.\n- \ud83d\udeab Przepisanie od zera, po kt\u00f3rym nikt nie wie, co si\u0119 zepsu\u0142o.\n- \ud83d\udeab Pi\u0119\u0107 warstw i fabryka dla CRUD-a na trzy pola.\n", "03-testing-and-verification": "# 03 \u2014 Testowanie i weryfikacja\n\n> Przykazanie III: *weryfikuj, nie deklaruj.* Testy to siatka odpalana w k\u00f3\u0142ko; weryfikacja to\n> zaobserwowany dow\u00f3d, \u017ce **ta** zmiana robi, co mia\u0142a. Oba s\u0105 obowi\u0105zkowe.\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Ka\u017cdy commit zmieniaj\u0105cy zachowanie niesie test.** Nowy endpoint \u2192 test trasy; nowy pr\u00f3g lub fallback \u2192\n   test progu; naprawiony bug \u2192 test, kt\u00f3ry najpierw go odtworzy\u0142. Zmiana zachowania bez testu to commit\n   **niekompletny**. TDD (czerwony \u2192 zielony \u2192 refaktor) to tryb domy\u015blny.\n2. **Poka\u017c dow\u00f3d, nie pro\u015b usera o sprawdzenie:** kod HTTP / redirect, potwierdzaj\u0105cy fragment HTML, liczby\n   z bazy (`integrity_check: ok`), screenshot przy zmianach wizualnych.\n3. **Smoke test sprawdza w\u0142a\u015bciw\u0105 stron\u0119, nie tylko `200`** \u2014 marker dowodz\u0105cy, \u017ce wyrenderowa\u0142a si\u0119\n   oczekiwana tre\u015b\u0107. Auth testuj z obu stron: anonim odbity, zalogowany widzi tre\u015b\u0107.\n4. **Po deployu smoke na \u017cywym serwerze** (przez `localhost` za flag\u0105 maintenance).\n5. **Raportuj uczciwie:** pora\u017cki z outputem; pomini\u0119te kroki jako pomini\u0119te; pora\u017ck\u0119 z dryfu danych\n   (nieaktualny fixture) nazwij po imieniu, z follow-upem \u2014 nie chowaj i nie traktuj jako blokady.\n6. **Liczby bierzesz u \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142a**, nie z pami\u0119ci ani z docs\u00f3w: `SELECT COUNT(*)` nie k\u0142amie.\n7. **Guard dowodzi incydent, nie fixture.** Check napisany po awarii odtwarzasz na **realnych danych** tej\n   awarii, zanim mu zaufasz \u2014 fixture dzieli martwe pole autora (check \u015bwie\u017co\u015bci przeszed\u0142 kiedy\u015b dziewi\u0119\u0107\n   zielonych test\u00f3w i przegapi\u0142 w\u0142asn\u0105 awari\u0119: `>` tam, gdzie trzeba by\u0142o `>=`). Potem zamro\u017a pr\u00f3g w fixture,\n   z testem **na** samym progu.\n8. **Testuj kontrakt, nie implementacj\u0119; suite szybki i deterministyczny** (izolowane I/O, seedy, testy\n   dziel\u0105ce port/baz\u0119 szeregowo). **CI bramkuje** merge i deploy.\n9. **Projekty webowe nios\u0105 domy\u015blnie:** meta SEO per strona, canonical + hreflang, JSON-LD, kontrast kolor\u00f3w\n   i nawigacj\u0119 klawiatur\u0105, **parytet j\u0119zykowy jako test, kt\u00f3ry pada**, brak martwych link\u00f3w wewn\u0119trznych.\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab \u201ePowinno dzia\u0142a\u0107\" jako konkluzja.\n- \ud83d\udeab Jeden test odpalony, \u201esuite zielony\" og\u0142oszony.\n- \ud83d\udeab Brak smoke po deployu, \u201ebo testy przesz\u0142y\".\n- \ud83d\udeab \u201eKompiluje si\u0119\" pomylone z \u201edzia\u0142a dla usera\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Os\u0142abienie testu albo guarda, \u017ceby zrobi\u0142 si\u0119 zielony.\n", "04-scripts-and-databases": "# 04 \u2014 Skrypty i bazy danych\n\n> Przykazania IV i V: *dry-run jest domy\u015blny; backup to rollback.*\n\n## Skrypty\n\n1. **Domy\u015blnie dry-run.** Skrypt zmieniaj\u0105cy dane wypisuje plan (co, ile wierszy, gdzie); \u015bwiadome\n   `--execute` zapisuje.\n2. **Idempotentny.** Dwa przebiegi daj\u0105 ten sam stan (`INSERT OR IGNORE` / `UPSERT`).\n3. **Wypisz, czego *nie* zrobi\u0142e\u015b** \u2014 uci\u0119cie, pr\u00f3bkowanie, pomini\u0119te wiersze. Cisza czyta si\u0119 jak \u201epokry\u0142em wszystko\".\n4. **Operacje mutuj\u0105ce katalog trzymaj poza automatycznymi pipeline'ami** (dedup, merge): dry-run \u2192 review \u2192 r\u0119czne `--execute`.\n5. **\u015arodowisko jest zapisane w `CLAUDE.md`** \u2014 interpreter, zmienne, venv. Agent nie zgaduje.\n6. **\u015acie\u017cki absolutne, gdy warto\u015b\u0107 przechodzi mi\u0119dzy narz\u0119dziami.** `/tmp` to jedno miejsce dla Git Bash,\n   inne dla Pythona na Windows, trzecie dla PowerShell \u2014 ka\u017cde narz\u0119dzie czyta wtedy *inny plik*. Przekazuj\n   pe\u0142ne \u015bcie\u017cki; gdy przebieg \u201enic nie zrobi\u0142\", wypisz `os.path.abspath(p)` / `pwd -W` i por\u00f3wnaj.\n\n## Bazy i migracje\n\n1. **Migracje forward-only.** Bez down-migracji; cofni\u0119cie schematu = przywr\u00f3cenie backupu.\n2. **Backup przed ka\u017cd\u0105 zmian\u0105 schematu/danych na prodzie**, z timestampem i retencj\u0105:\n   `sqlite3 data/app.db \".backup backups/pre-deploy-$(date +%F_%H%M%S).db\"`\n3. **Snapshot przez `.backup` / `VACUUM INTO`, nigdy `cp`/`scp` \u017cywej bazy** \u2014 WAL robi kopi\u0119 niesp\u00f3jn\u0105.\n4. **Additive przed destructive.** `ADD COLUMN` jest bezpieczne; `DROP`/`RENAME` czeka na p\u00f3\u017aniejszy deploy,\n   gdy \u017caden dzia\u0142aj\u0105cy kod nie czyta kolumny.\n5. **Integralno\u015b\u0107 po ka\u017cdej operacji na danych:** `integrity_check`, `foreign_key_check`, liczby wierszy.\n   Odr\u00f3\u017cnij szum zastany (jest przed i po) od regresji (nowe naruszenia).\n6. **Masow\u0105 albo nieodwracaln\u0105 mutacj\u0119 prze\u0107wicz na \u015bwie\u017cej kopii proda** \u2014 dry-run pokazuje plan, nie wynik.\n   `.backup` proda \u2192 przebieg na kopii \u2192 sprawd\u017a liczby, integralno\u015b\u0107 i trudne przypadki \u2192 dopiero prod.\n   Brat tej regu\u0142y na poziomie deployu \u2192 [18](18-release-rehearsal.md).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Skrypt mutuj\u0105cy bez `--dry-run`.\n- \ud83d\udeab Migracja wdro\u017cona bez backupu \u201ebo additive\".\n- \ud83d\udeab `DROP` kolumny, kt\u00f3r\u0105 czyta dzia\u0142aj\u0105cy kod.\n- \ud83d\udeab `cp app.db` przy dzia\u0142aj\u0105cym serwerze.\n- \ud83d\udeab Krok mutuj\u0105cy katalog wpi\u0119ty w automatyczny pipeline.\n", "05-git-and-deployments": "# 05 \u2014 Git i wdro\u017cenia\n\n> Przykazania II, VI, VIII, IX. Git to pami\u0119\u0107 projektu i przycisk cofania dla ca\u0142ej reszty; polityka\n> deployu to miejsce, gdzie b\u0142\u0105d kosztuje najwi\u0119cej. Pr\u00f3ba na preprodzie \u2192 [18](18-release-rehearsal.md);\n> swap bazy proda \u2192 [19](19-database-swap-runbook.md).\n\n## Nawyki w gicie\n\n1. **Szukaj w historii, zanim zaimplementujesz:**\n   ```bash\n   git log -S\"symbol\" --oneline     # gdzie narodzi\u0142 si\u0119 symbol/flaga/kolumna\n   git log -G\"regex\" --oneline      # commity, kt\u00f3rych diff pasuje\n   git log --grep=\"s\u0142owo\" --oneline # opisy commit\u00f3w\n   git blame <plik> -L a,b          # kto/kiedy/dlaczego\n   ```\n2. **Trzymaj `git status` czysty.** Jeden temat na commit. \u015amieci (`.bak`, pliki tymczasowe, przypadkowe\n   bazy) wcze\u015bnie do `.gitignore` (komentarz `#` w osobnej linii). Plik widoczny jako zmieniony przy pustym\n   `git diff HEAD` to szum CRLF, nie praca \u2014 nie commituj go.\n3. **Sieroty:** niezacommitowana praca, kt\u00f3rej nie pisa\u0142e\u015b (innej sesji), **nigdy nie trafia do Twojego\n   commita ani deployu** bez potwierdzenia \u2014 zapytaj, czy to sko\u0144czona praca, czy eksperyment.\n4. **Opisy commit\u00f3w po angielsku**, co + dlaczego, `refs #N` / `fixes #N`.\n\n## Cofanie: najprawdopodobniej tego nie straci\u0142e\u015b\n\nZacommitowana praca jest do odzyskania; git pami\u0119ta ka\u017cd\u0105 pozycj\u0119 ga\u0142\u0119zi (~90 dni). **Commituj wcze\u015bnie** \u2014\ncommit jest darmowy, prywatny do pushu i jest Twoim punktem zapisu.\n\n| Sytuacja | Droga powrotna |\n|---|---|\n| Rozwalony plik, bez commita | `git restore <plik>` |\n| Cofnij ostatni commit, zostaw prac\u0119 | `git reset --soft HEAD~1` |\n| Commit na z\u0142ej ga\u0142\u0119zi | `git branch <w\u0142a\u015bciwa>` tutaj, potem `git reset --hard HEAD~1` na z\u0142ej |\n| Skasowana ga\u0142\u0105\u017a / \u201ezgubione\" commity | `git reflog` \u2192 znajd\u017a hash \u2192 `git branch <nazwa> <hash>` |\n| Merge poszed\u0142 \u017ale (bez pushu) | `git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD` |\n| Potrzebny plik w dawnej wersji | `git show <commit>:<\u015bcie\u017cka>` \u00b7 `git restore --source=<commit> -- <\u015bcie\u017cka>` |\n\nTylko trzy komendy niszcz\u0105 prac\u0119 \u2014 i tylko **niezacommitowan\u0105**: `git reset --hard`, `git restore .` /\n`git checkout .`, `git clean -fd`. Git nie ma dla nich dry-runu: **wcze\u015bniejszy commit jest dry-runem** (\u2192 IV).\n\n## Praca r\u00f3wnoleg\u0142a: issues i kr\u00f3tkie ga\u0142\u0119zie\n\n\u201eSolo\" to i tak wielu pisz\u0105cych: jeden developer rutynowo odpala **kilka sesji Claude** na jednym repo.\n\u201eDrug\u0105 stron\u0105\" jest zwykle inna sesja \u2014 czytaj \u201ewsp\u00f3\u0142pracownik\" jako \u201einna sesja\".\n\n1. **Jedno zadanie = jeden issue**: tytu\u0142 = rezultat, tre\u015b\u0107 = kontekst + kryteria zaliczenia. Numer issue\n   spina ga\u0142\u0105\u017a, commity i PR; zamyka go **PR/merge**, nie commit na ga\u0142\u0119zi roboczej.\n2. **Kr\u00f3tkie ga\u0142\u0119zie** (`feat/NN-koszyk`, `fix/NN-logowanie`) \u017cyj\u0105ce godziny\u2013dni; ma\u0142e PR-y; merge, kasuj,\n   synchronizuj si\u0119 z `main` cz\u0119sto.\n3. **Diff przed mergem do ga\u0142\u0119zi deployu** \u2014 merge wnosi *ka\u017cdy* commit ze \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142a, tak\u017ce prac\u0119 innych sesji:\n   `git log <deploy>..<\u017ar\u00f3d\u0142o> --oneline`. Pilny izolowany fix idzie **cherry-pickiem**.\n4. **Po ka\u017cdym cherry-picku sprawd\u017a wynik:** `git diff <baza-deployu> HEAD --stat` ma pokaza\u0107 tylko Twoj\u0105\n   zmian\u0119 \u2014 fallback 3-way potrafi wci\u0105gn\u0105\u0107 cudzy hunk (zab\u0142\u0105kany `require` \u2192 crash przy starcie \u2192 502).\n5. **Jedna r\u00f3wnoleg\u0142a sesja = jeden `git worktree`**, nigdy dwie sesje w jednym checkoucie (\u2192 [16](16-driving-claude.md)).\n\n**Branching ro\u015bnie z projektem \u2014 ka\u017cdy szczebel dok\u0142adasz, gdy wymusi go metryka:**\n1. Solo, brak \u017cywych user\u00f3w \u2192 sam `main`, commitowany z dyscyplin\u0105.\n2. Pierwsze wdro\u017cenie produkcyjne \u2192 ka\u017cda zmiana na kr\u00f3tkiej ga\u0142\u0119zi, przejrzana, zmergowana do zawsze-wdra\u017calnego `main`, deploy otagowany.\n3. Wi\u0119cej os\u00f3b / r\u00f3wnoleg\u0142e nurty \u2192 `develop` do integracji, wydania `develop \u2192 main`, najpierw sprawdzone\n   na preprodzie \u2014 kopii proda (\u2192 [18](18-release-rehearsal.md)).\n\nKa\u017cda zmiana trafia na `main` przejrzana \u2014 solo recenzentem jest \u015bwiadomy drugi przegl\u0105d diffu.\n\n## Wdro\u017cenia \u2014 polityka nadrz\u0119dna\n\n> \u26d4 **Nigdy nie deployuj na prod automatycznie.** Pull na serwerze, reload, migracja, swap bazy, flaga\n> maintenance \u2014 **tylko na wyra\u017ane \u201ewdra\u017caj\" u\u017cytkownika.** Mo\u017cesz zaproponowa\u0107 deploy; czekasz na \u201etak\".\n> Commit i push to nie deploy. Ta regu\u0142a bije ka\u017cdy tryb auto.\n\n1. **Serwer wdra\u017ca `origin/<ga\u0142\u0105\u017a>`, nie Tw\u00f3j laptop.** Przed ka\u017cdym deployem: `git fetch`, potem\n   `git log origin/<g>..<g>` (niewypchni\u0119te commity \u2192 najpierw push) i `git log <g>..origin/<g>` (inna sesja\n   wypchn\u0119\u0142a \u2192 najpierw pull).\n2. **Boot-check na celu przed reloadem:** po pullu uruchom nowy kod w jednorazowym procesie\n   (`node -e \"require('./src/app')\"`, z `listen` za `require.main === module`); reload **tylko** na jego\n   marker OK. Crash zostawia wtedy stary proces w pracy.\n3. **Dwa typy deployu.** Sam kod \u2192 pull + boot-check + reload bez przestoju. Migracja / zmiana zale\u017cno\u015bci /\n   swap bazy \u2192 **okno maintenance** (firmowa strona 503), \u017ceby nic nie dzia\u0142a\u0142o na p\u00f3\u0142-zmigrowanym schemacie.\n4. **Taguj ka\u017cdy deploy:** `git tag -a deploy-$(date +%F) -m \"<co idzie live>\"` + `git push --tags`.\n   Co jest live: `git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match 'deploy-*'`. Rollback kodu = checkout tagu + reload;\n   rollback schematu = przywr\u00f3cenie backupu.\n5. **Ka\u017cdy deploy aktualizuje publiczny changelog** prostym j\u0119zykiem \u2014 co user zyskuje.\n6. **Ka\u017cdy incydent na prodzie staje si\u0119 datowan\u0105, numerowan\u0105 lekcj\u0105 w runbooku** (`docs/`), czytan\u0105 przed\n   kolejnym deployem (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n7. **Pierwsi realni userzy = nocny backup bazy, tagi deploy\u00f3w i firmowa strona maintenance \u2014 wszystko pierwszego dnia ruchu.**\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Auto-deploy, \u201egotowe, wi\u0119c idzie\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Deploy bez uzgodnienia lokalne \u21c4 `origin` \u2014 niewypchni\u0119te commity po cichu nie jad\u0105.\n- \ud83d\udeab Merge wsp\u00f3lnej ga\u0142\u0119zi do ga\u0142\u0119zi deployu bez przeczytania, co wnosi.\n- \ud83d\udeab D\u0142ugo \u017cyj\u0105ca ga\u0142\u0105\u017a \u201ewielki refaktor\"; PR \u201ewszystko naraz\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Praca bez issue \u2014 po miesi\u0105cu nikt nie wie \u201edlaczego\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Brak tagu deployu \u2014 \u201eco jest live?\" staje si\u0119 zgadywank\u0105.\n- \ud83d\udeab Niezmiennik skryptu deployu bez testu (`$(date)` rozwini\u0119ty raz \u2192 ka\u017cdy backup nadpisuje ten sam plik).\n", "06-collaboration-and-memory": "# 06 \u2014 Wsp\u00f3\u0142praca i pami\u0119\u0107\n\n> Przykazania VI i X: jak u\u017cytkownik i agent pracuj\u0105 razem, gdzie biegnie granica i jak nic nie ginie\n> mi\u0119dzy sesjami.\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Plan \u2192 szkic \u2192 skala.** Poka\u017c 3 przyk\u0142ady, zbierz feedback, potem zr\u00f3b 10, potem 100 \u2014 nigdy odwrotnie.\n2. **M\u00f3w w natywnym j\u0119zyku u\u017cytkownika** i zapisz go (oraz jego poziom techniczny) raz w `CLAUDE.md`\n   (\u2192 [07](07-new-project-day-0.md)). Kod i opisy commit\u00f3w zostaj\u0105 po angielsku; nazwy, kt\u00f3re drukuje\n   narz\u0119dzie, po angielsku w ka\u017cdym j\u0119zyku (\u2192 [17](17-language-and-guards.md)).\n3. **Sp\u00f3jrz na cel, zanim skasujesz albo nadpiszesz.** Je\u015bli przeczy opisowi albo nie Ty go stworzy\u0142e\u015b \u2014\n   zg\u0142o\u015b, nie kasuj.\n4. **Zgoda jest per akcja.** \u201eWdr\u00f3\u017c X\" to nie \u201ewdra\u017caj, kiedy chcesz\". Opublikowana tre\u015b\u0107 mo\u017ce zosta\u0107\n   w cache'u lub indeksie nawet po usuni\u0119ciu.\n5. **Pami\u0119\u0107 trzyma tylko to, czego nie utrzyma repo** \u2014 kim jest user, jego feedback z \u201edlaczego\",\n   ograniczenia niewidoczne w kodzie. U\u017cywaj wbudowanej pami\u0119ci agenta; nigdy nie kopiuj do niej fakt\u00f3w z repo.\n\n## Mapa uprawnie\u0144\n\nCa\u0142a granica w jednym miejscu \u2014 zatwierdzana raz w Dniu 0, czytana co sesj\u0119:\n\n| \u2705 Wolno \u2014 r\u00f3b | \u23f8\ufe0f Zapytaj, czekaj na \u201etak\" | \u26d4 Nigdy \u2014 kto by nie prosi\u0142 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Czytaj/grepuj cokolwiek w repo | Deploy na prod, w ka\u017cdej postaci (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)) | Wymy\u015bli\u0107 dane prawne/to\u017csamo\u015bci \u2014 NIP, adres, nazw\u0119 firmy (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)) |\n| Odpalaj testy, lintery, buildy | Push/publikacja do zdalnego repo | Zadeklarowa\u0107 praktyk\u0119, kt\u00f3rej kod nie realizuje (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)) |\n| Commituj lokalnie, cz\u0119sto, ma\u0142o | Migracja albo swap bazy na prodzie (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)) | Zacommitowa\u0107 sekrety (\u2192 [08](08-stack-and-technologies.md)) |\n| Tw\u00f3rz/kasuj **w\u0142asne** ga\u0142\u0119zie | Wysy\u0142ka maili/wiadomo\u015bci do realnych ludzi | Skasowa\u0107 backup, kt\u00f3ry jest czyim\u015b rollbackiem (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)) |\n| Odpalaj skrypty w **dry-run** | Kasowanie/nadpisanie czego\u015b, czego nie tworzy\u0142e\u015b | Os\u0142abi\u0107 test albo guard, \u017ceby zrobi\u0142 si\u0119 zielony (\u2192 [03](03-testing-and-verification.md)) |\n| Pisz/aktualizuj docsy, `AI_README`, plany | Do\u0142o\u017cenie p\u0142atnej us\u0142ugi lub zale\u017cno\u015bci (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)) | Zaraportowa\u0107 \u201edzia\u0142a\" bez dowodu (\u2192 [00](00-commandments.md) III) |\n| Refaktoruj w uzgodnionym zakresie | Zmiana schedulera/crona albo configu serwera (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)) | Wmie\u015b\u0107 niezacommitowan\u0105 prac\u0119 innej sesji do commita (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)) |\n| D\u0142ugie joby w tle, lokalnie | Tykanie realnych danych u\u017cytkownik\u00f3w (\u2192 [00](00-commandments.md) VII) | Wdro\u017cy\u0107 migracj\u0119 bez zrobionego backupu (\u2192 [00](00-commandments.md) V) |\n\nLewa kolumna jest **celowo szeroka** \u2014 to ona czyni autopilota bezpiecznym, a nie przyklepywanym. \u201eTak\"\nw \u015brodkowej kolumnie zu\u017cywa si\u0119 na t\u0119 jedn\u0105 akcj\u0119. Prawa kolumna si\u0119 nie rusza: \u201euser poprosi\u0142\" nie jest tam\nargumentem \u2014 powiedz dlaczego, zaproponuj uczciw\u0105 alternatyw\u0119.\n\n## Domknij jednostk\u0119 pracy podsumowaniem\n\nSkanowalne podsumowanie, nie proza \u2014 najlepiej widget dopasowany do poziomu usera:\n**co si\u0119 zmieni\u0142o** \u00b7 **dow\u00f3d** (liczby test\u00f3w, kody HTTP, liczby) \u00b7 **commit** (hash, opis) \u00b7\n**nast\u0119pne kroki po kolei** (docsy \u2192 plany \u2192 deploy, deploy wci\u0105\u017c za wyra\u017anym \u201etak\").\n\n## Przekazanie stanu, gdy ko\u0144czy si\u0119 kontekst\n\nD\u0142uga sesja zostaje podsumowana, a podsumowanie gubi *dlaczego* i p\u00f3\u0142-gotowy stan w drzewie.\n\n1. **Dowo\u017a prac\u0119 w commitach, nie w rozmowie** \u2014 commit prze\u017cyje kompaktowanie, p\u00f3\u0142-gotowa edycja staje si\u0119 sierot\u0105.\n2. **Zapisz decyzj\u0119 tam, gdzie mieszka, w chwili jej podj\u0119cia** (`CLAUDE.md`, ADR, `docs/plans/`, `AI_README`).\n3. **Zostaw czytelny `git status`** \u2014 to *jest* przekazanie dla nast\u0119pnej sesji.\n4. **Gdy zbli\u017ca si\u0119 sufit, domknij bie\u017c\u0105c\u0105 jednostk\u0119, zanim otworzysz nast\u0119pn\u0105.**\n\nTest: czy nast\u0119pna sesja podnios\u0142aby to z samego repo, bez tej rozmowy?\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Skalowanie przed review.\n- \ud83d\udeab Kasowanie albo nadpisanie bez spojrzenia na cel.\n- \ud83d\udeab Jednorazowa zgoda potraktowana jako trwa\u0142a.\n- \ud83d\udeab Pami\u0119\u0107 jako wysypisko fakt\u00f3w z repo.\n- \ud83d\udeab Ko\u0144czenie zmiany \u015bcian\u0105 prozy zamiast statusu + commita + nast\u0119pnych krok\u00f3w.\n", "07-new-project-day-0": "# 07 \u2014 Nowy projekt: Dzie\u0144 0\n\n> Checklista, kt\u00f3r\u0105 Claude wykonuje na starcie projektu \u2014 **jedyna** checklista w doktrynie; inne rozdzia\u0142y\n> tu linkuj\u0105. Cel: ka\u017cda kolejna sesja ma grunt pod nogami.\n\n## Wepnij The Craft (raz)\n\n1. **Umie\u015b\u0107 regu\u0142y w `docs/rules/`** \u2014 jako submodu\u0142 (aktualizowalny) albo zwyk\u0142a kopia:\n   `git submodule add https://github.com/JKolada/TheCraftWithClaude docs/rules`\n2. **Wstaw zwyk\u0142y wska\u017anik na g\u00f3r\u0119 `CLAUDE.md` i `AGENTS.md`** (utw\u00f3rz, je\u015bli ich brak). Widzisz `docs/rules/`,\n   a wska\u017anika nie ma \u2014 dodaj go, nie czekaj, a\u017c kto\u015b poprosi:\n   > This project follows the build rules in `docs/rules/` (The Craft); they override default behavior.\n   > Read `docs/rules/00-commandments.md` first, then `grep -i <keyword> docs/rules/AI_README.md` and read\n   > only the matched chapter. This `CLAUDE.md` remains the project's constitution.\n\n   Wska\u017anik, nie wklejka \u2014 regu\u0142y \u017cyj\u0105 w jednym miejscu. Bez etykiet `SYSTEM` (\u2192 [00](00-commandments.md)).\n3. **Wybierz j\u0119zyk regu\u0142** \u2014 angielski w rootcie, polski w `pl/`.\n\n## 0. Brief produktowy \u2014 zapytaj, zanim cokolwiek powstanie\n\nPytaj kr\u00f3tkimi grupami; gdy user nie wie, zaproponuj default i **oznacz go jako za\u0142o\u017cenie**:\n\n- **Poziom techniczny i styl raportowania** (pytaj pierwszy \u2014 ustawia rejestr: nietechniczny \u2192 bez \u017cargonu,\n  pytania zamkni\u0119te z rekomendacj\u0105).\n- **J\u0119zyki, kt\u00f3rymi user m\u00f3wi; technologie, kt\u00f3re zna.**\n- **J\u0119zyki i rynki docelowe** (i18n od pierwszego dnia? RTL? waluty) \u2192 [10](10-seo-and-translations.md).\n- **MVP vs p\u00f3\u017aniej; konta i role; integracje** (p\u0142atno\u015bci, AI, mail, mapy).\n- **Monetyzacja** (darmowe / p\u0142atne / subskrypcja / reklamy; kiedy; dostawca; faktury/VAT) \u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md).\n- **Kierunek wizualny i odczucie** (istniej\u0105ca marka? jasny/ciemny? bud\u017cet animacji, `prefers-reduced-motion`, WCAG?).\n- **Propozycja warto\u015bci i ton** (jedno zdanie; nazwa i domena ustalone?).\n\nOdpowied\u017a l\u0105duje jako **`project-brief.md` w rootcie projektu** (obok `docs/rules/`, nie w \u015brodku) albo\nwklejona jako pierwsza wiadomo\u015b\u0107. Generuje go widok briefu w czytniku.\n\n## Checklista\n\n1. **Rekonesans:** przeczytaj istniej\u0105ce `CLAUDE.md` / `AI_README` / `README`; `git log --oneline -20`,\n   `git status` (sieroty, \u015bmieci); ustal stack, komend\u0119 uruchomienia, gdzie le\u017c\u0105 dane.\n2. **`CLAUDE.md`** z briefu (szkielet ni\u017cej): stack + dok\u0142adne komendy, polityki, j\u0119zyk i poziom usera,\n   mapa uprawnie\u0144 (\u2192 [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md)), datowany \u201eStan bie\u017c\u0105cy\".\n3. **Polityka j\u0119zykowa** \u2014 dwie linie w `CLAUDE.md`: kod po angielsku (identyfikatory, testy, baza, CLI,\n   klucze), proza w j\u0119zyku projektu (\u2192 [17](17-language-and-guards.md)).\n4. **Higiena repo:** `.gitignore` (zale\u017cno\u015bci, lokalne bazy, `*.bak`, generowane, temp); konwencja commit\u00f3w\n   i tag\u00f3w deployu; zadania w GitHub Issues / `docs/plans/`.\n5. **`AI_README.md` w ka\u017cdym katalogu z kodem**; `docs/` na architektur\u0119 i model danych.\n6. **Testy:** szkielet test\u00f3w + smoke z markerami; Playwright na osobnym porcie, je\u015bli jest UI;\n   **oba stra\u017cniki z [17](17-language-and-guards.md) w pierwszym commicie**, baseline zacommitowany.\n7. **Skille:** `/run` + smoke, `/run-tests`, `/update-ai-readme`, `/add-migration` (\u2192 [02](02-skills-and-refactoring.md)).\n8. **Dane:** migracje forward-only, katalog `backups/` z retencj\u0105, domy\u015blny dry-run (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n9. **Przed pierwszym realnym userem:** globalny handler b\u0142\u0119d\u00f3w i 500 bez wycieku, timeouty na ka\u017cdym\n   zewn\u0119trznym wywo\u0142aniu, kwoty na p\u0142atnych endpointach, trwa\u0142y store sesji we w\u0142asnym pliku (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md));\n   wywo\u0142ania AI z kontraktem wyj\u015bcia i cache'em (\u2192 [15](15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots.md)).\n10. **Produkcja (przygotowana, nie odpalona):** runbook deployu, firmowa strona maintenance, plan rollbacku\n    (tag + backup), publiczny changelog, nocny backup od pierwszego realnego usera (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n11. **Prawo:** regulamin, polityka prywatno\u015bci i disclaimery z placeholderami `[do uzupe\u0142nienia]`; zgoda przed\n    trackerami (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n12. **Baseline'y:** Lighthouse po pierwszej dzia\u0142aj\u0105cej stronie; indeksy na kolumnach filtr\u00f3w od pierwszej migracji (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)).\n13. **Decyzje architektoniczne** tylko tam, gdzie odchodzisz od domy\u015blnego stacku \u2014 po jednym ADR (\u2192 [08](08-stack-and-technologies.md), [12](12-flexibility-and-scalability.md)).\n\n**Minimalny Dzie\u0144 0:** brief \u2192 `CLAUDE.md` \u2192 `.gitignore` \u2192 `/run` ze smoke testem \u2192 szkielet test\u00f3w ze\nstra\u017cnikami \u2192 `backups/`. Reszta ro\u015bnie z projektem.\n\n## Szkielet `CLAUDE.md`\n\n```\n# CLAUDE.md \u2014 <Projekt>\n<wska\u017anik na docs/rules/ \u2014 patrz wy\u017cej>\n\n## Czym to jest\n<jeden akapit: co robi, dla kogo>\n\n## Stack i jak uruchomi\u0107\n<warstwa \u2192 technologia \u2192 lokalizacja>\nuruchom: <dok\u0142adna komenda>\n\n## Polityki (nadpisuj\u0105 domy\u015blne zachowanie)\n- Nigdy deploy/push bez wyra\u017anego \u201ewdra\u017caj\". Commituj swobodnie; nieodwracalne potwierdzaj (mapa uprawnie\u0144: docs/rules/06).\n- Domy\u015blnie dry-run. Prod jest \u015bwi\u0119ty; dane user\u00f3w nienaruszalne.\n- M\u00f3w do mnie po <j\u0119zyk>; znam <j\u0119zyki / technologie>.\n- Kod: angielski (identyfikatory, testy, baza, CLI, klucze). Proza: <j\u0119zyk>.\n- <twarde regu\u0142y projektu: prywatno\u015b\u0107, ograniczenia domeny>\n\n## Stan bie\u017c\u0105cy (<data>)\n- <liczby, zastosowane migracje, co jest live>\n```\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Kod przed briefem \u2014 j\u0119zyk, monetyzacja i marka do\u0142o\u017cone p\u00f3\u017aniej to przepisanie.\n- \ud83d\udeab Trzydzie\u015bci pyta\u0144 naraz \u2014 pytaj o to, co zmienia plan, reszt\u0119 proponuj jako defaulty.\n- \ud83d\udeab Ciche za\u0142o\u017cenia (\u201epewnie tylko po polsku\", \u201epewnie za darmo\").\n- \ud83d\udeab \u017bargon wobec nietechnicznego usera.\n", "08-stack-and-technologies": "# 08 \u2014 Stack i technologie\n\n> Z\u0142ota zasada altytudy w doborze narz\u0119dzi: *najprostsze, co spe\u0142nia dzisiejsze wymaganie*; z\u0142o\u017cono\u015b\u0107\n> dok\u0142adasz, gdy wymusi j\u0105 metryka (\u2192 [12](12-flexibility-and-scalability.md)).\n\n## Defaulty (odchodzisz \u015bwiadomie, z ADR)\n\n| Warstwa | Default | Zmie\u0144, gdy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agent / proces | **Claude Code** + **Git** + **GitHub** (repo, PR-y, Issues) | \u2014 |\n| Backend, skrypty, dane | **Python** (FastAPI na API, pytest) \u2014 najszerszy ekosystem do skrypt\u00f3w, danych i spinania AI | zadanie lepiej robi inny j\u0119zyk: **Go** (wsp\u00f3\u0142bie\u017cne us\u0142ugi sieciowe, jedna statyczna binarka), **Rust** (gor\u0105ce \u015bcie\u017cki, bezpiecze\u0144stwo pami\u0119ci, ma\u0142y narzut), **Kotlin** (biblioteki JVM, Android), **TypeScript/Node** (warstwa web ju\u017c jest w JS) |\n| Baza | **SQLite** (WAL) | wsp\u00f3\u0142bie\u017cne zapisy, role, ci\u0119\u017ckie relacje \u2192 **PostgreSQL** |\n| Web / front | HTML renderowany po stronie serwera + troch\u0119 czystego JS | naprawd\u0119 bogata interakcja \u2192 framework (np. Next.js) |\n| API | REST/JSON z jawnym kontraktem (OpenAPI) | \u2014 |\n| Cache / kolejka | brak | zmierzony profil ruchu \u2192 Redis |\n| Pakowanie | Docker tam, gdzie zale\u017cno\u015bci bol\u0105 | skrypt bez zale\u017cno\u015bci \u2192 bez kontenera |\n| Hosting | **jeden VPS** (np. Hetzner) + nginx + `systemd`/`pm2` \u2014 **monolit** | nier\u00f3wny/globalny ruch, cold start akceptowalny \u2192 serverless |\n| Ikony | **tylko Lucide** (jedna geometria, jeden sprite) | \u2014 |\n| LLM | Claude przez oficjalne SDK, streamowany (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)) | \u2014 |\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Monolit to domy\u015blna architektura:** jedna aplikacja, jedna baza, jeden serwer \u2014 jeden deploy, jeden\n   log, jeden backup, jeden rollback. Na us\u0142ugi dzielisz dopiero, gdy wymaga tego ruch, zesp\u00f3\u0142 albo izolacja awarii.\n2. **J\u0119zyk wybierasz wg dopasowania, nie z przyzwyczajenia.** Python to default, nie dogmat: zmie\u0144 go, gdy\n   decyduje wydajno\u015b\u0107, wsp\u00f3\u0142bie\u017cno\u015b\u0107, spos\u00f3b wdro\u017cenia albo biblioteka, kt\u00f3ra istnieje tylko w innym\n   ekosystemie \u2014 i zapisz w ADR, co przes\u0105dzi\u0142o (liczba albo brakuj\u0105ca biblioteka). Jeden j\u0119zyk na warstw\u0119,\n   nie jeden na humor; przepisanie \u201edla szybko\u015bci\" wymaga najpierw pomiaru (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)).\n3. **Testowalne od pierwszej linii bije modne.** Technologia, kt\u00f3rej nie pokryjesz szybkimi,\n   deterministycznymi testami, to z\u0142y wyb\u00f3r (\u2192 [03](03-testing-and-verification.md)).\n4. **Nudne i dojrza\u0142e ponad nowe:** decyduj\u0105 ekosystem, dokumentacja i testowalno\u015b\u0107, nie hype.\n5. **Odtwarzalne \u015brodowiska:** przypi\u0119te zale\u017cno\u015bci (lock), wirtualne \u015brodowisko / obraz.\n6. **Sekrety \u017cyj\u0105 w env albo w sejfie na sekrety \u2014 nigdy w repo.**\n7. **Ka\u017cdy niedomy\u015blny wyb\u00f3r dostaje ADR:** dlaczego i odrzucone alternatywy, w `docs/`.\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Mikroserwisy, Kubernetes albo rozproszona baza na MVP.\n- \ud83d\udeab Stack wybrany pod CV.\n- \ud83d\udeab Ci\u0119\u017ckie SPA tam, gdzie wystarczy HTML z serwera.\n- \ud83d\udeab Mieszanie bibliotek ikon.\n- \ud83d\udeab Nieprzypi\u0119te zale\u017cno\u015bci \u2014 \u201ewczoraj dzia\u0142a\u0142o\".\n- \ud83d\udeab Python wci\u015bni\u0119ty w zadanie, kt\u00f3remu \u017ale s\u0142u\u017cy (gor\u0105ca \u015bcie\u017cka, wsp\u00f3\u0142bie\u017cna us\u0142uga, biblioteka \u017cyj\u0105ca na JVM) \u2014 albo przepisanie na \u201eszybszy\" j\u0119zyk bez pomiaru.\n", "09-law-and-protecting-the-creator": "# 09 \u2014 Prawo i ochrona tw\u00f3rcy\n\n> Przykazania VI i VII na zewn\u0105trz: solowy tw\u00f3rca albo jednoosobowa dzia\u0142alno\u015b\u0107 wypuszczaj\u0105ca publiczn\u0105\n> aplikacj\u0119 **odpowiada osobi\u015bcie**. Regulamin, polityka prywatno\u015bci i disclaimery to jego zbroja \u2014 pisana\n> tak starannie jak migracja na prodzie. \u26a0\ufe0f Higiena in\u017cynierska, nie porada prawna: przed wi\u0105zaniem realnych\n> user\u00f3w na skal\u0119 \u2014 prawnik.\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Nigdy nie wymy\u015blaj danych prawnych ani to\u017csamo\u015bci.** Nazwa firmy, NIP, adres = placeholdery\n   `[do uzupe\u0142nienia]` wype\u0142niane przez w\u0142a\u015bciciela. Widoczny placeholder bije wiarygodnie wygl\u0105daj\u0105cy fa\u0142sz.\n2. **Nigdy nie deklaruj praktyki, kt\u00f3rej kod nie realizuje.** \u201eAnonimizujemy\", \u201eusuwamy po N dniach\", \u201enie\n   czytamy rozm\u00f3w\" musi by\u0107 prawd\u0105 w kodzie \u2014 najlepiej dowiedzion\u0105 testem (\u2192 [11](11-data-model-and-normalization.md)).\n3. **Jeden operator, nazwany identycznie wsz\u0119dzie:** regulamin, polityka, stopka, faktury, UI.\n4. **Regulamin zawiera:** operatora; czym us\u0142uga **jest, a czym nie** (np. \u201epor\u00f3wnywarka, nie sprzedawca\";\n   \u201einformacja, nie porada medyczna/prawna/finansowa\"); obowi\u0105zki usera (legalne u\u017cycie, limit wieku);\n   ograniczenie odpowiedzialno\u015bci; moderacj\u0119 i usuwanie kont; IP (user zachowuje tre\u015bci, udziela licencji);\n   **prawo w\u0142a\u015bciwe Twojej jurysdykcji**, reklamacje, spos\u00f3b og\u0142aszania zmian, kontakt.\n5. **Polityka prywatno\u015bci zawiera:** administratora; ka\u017cd\u0105 kategori\u0119 danych z celem, podstaw\u0105 prawn\u0105\n   i retencj\u0105; podmioty przetwarzaj\u0105ce; prawa usera (dost\u0119p, usuni\u0119cie, eksport, sprzeciw); cookies; wiek; kontakt.\n6. **Zgoda przed trackerami.** Skrypty analityczne i marketingowe \u0142aduj\u0105 si\u0119 dopiero po zgodzie.\n7. **Disclaimery pasuj\u0105 do domeny:** bramka wieku dla tre\u015bci z ograniczeniem wiekowym; \u201eto nie porada\" dla\n   zdrowia/prawa/finans\u00f3w; zasoby kryzysowe per region dla produkt\u00f3w wra\u017cliwych (\u2192 [10](10-seo-and-translations.md)).\n8. **Regulamin \u2194 polityka \u2194 UI \u2194 kod m\u00f3wi\u0105 jedno.** Sprzeczno\u015b\u0107 mi\u0119dzy warstwami jest gorsza ni\u017c brak klauzuli.\n9. **Dokumenty prawne wchodz\u0105 do Dnia 0 jako placeholdery**, nie \u201ep\u00f3\u017aniej\" (\u2192 [07](07-new-project-day-0.md)).\n\n## Checklista bezpiecze\u0144stwa (indeks)\n\nJedne drzwi; ka\u017cda regu\u0142a jest egzekwowana tam, gdzie \u017cyje.\n\n- **Sekrety** w env / sejfie, rotowane po wycieku (\u2192 [08](08-stack-and-technologies.md)).\n- **Auth i sesje:** bcrypt albo OAuth, nag\u0142\u00f3wki bezpiecze\u0144stwa, rate-limit; sesje w trwa\u0142ym store (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n- **Autoryzacja po stronie serwera:** *ten* user mo\u017ce dotkn\u0105\u0107 *tego* wiersza/akcji \u2014 nie tylko \u201ezalogowany\" (IDOR).\n- **Wej\u015bcie jest wrogie:** waliduj kszta\u0142t, parametryzuj SQL, tre\u015b\u0107 zewn\u0119trzn\u0105 i usera traktuj jako\n  **dane, nie instrukcje** (\u2192 [15](15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots.md)).\n- **B\u0142\u0119dy nie wyciekaj\u0105:** \u017cadnych stack trace'\u00f3w, sekret\u00f3w ani PII do user\u00f3w ani do log\u00f3w (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n- **Kwoty** na p\u0142atnych/drogich endpointach (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Wymy\u015blony NIP, adres albo nazwa firmy w produkcie.\n- \ud83d\udeab Copy obiecuj\u0105ce wi\u0119cej, ni\u017c produkt robi (\u201ew pe\u0142ni anonimowo\", \u201egwarantowane ceny\").\n- \ud83d\udeab Regulamin i polityka z innym operatorem albo inn\u0105 retencj\u0105.\n- \ud83d\udeab Strona prawna w jednym j\u0119zyku w produkcie wieloj\u0119zycznym.\n- \ud83d\udeab Analityka \u0142adowana przed zgod\u0105.\n", "10-seo-and-translations": "# 10 \u2014 SEO i t\u0142umaczenia\n\n> Przykazanie III zastosowane do widoczno\u015bci: indeksowalne, kompletne w ka\u017cdym j\u0119zyku, zmierzone \u2014 nie\n> \u201echyba OK\". Standardowe SEO strony (unikalny title i description, canonical, sitemap, `robots.txt`, jedno\n> `h1`, czyste slugi, karty OG) jest za\u0142o\u017cone; tu s\u0105 regu\u0142y, kt\u00f3re \u0142atwo zepsu\u0107.\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Wieloj\u0119zyczne = wzajemne `hreflang` dla ka\u017cdej pary j\u0119zyk\u00f3w + `x-default`.** Bez tego wyszukiwarka\n   serwuje z\u0142y j\u0119zyk.\n2. **Kompletno\u015b\u0107 j\u0119zyk\u00f3w to test, kt\u00f3ry pada:** brakuj\u0105cy klucz albo strona w dowolnym j\u0119zyku psuje build (\u2192 [03](03-testing-and-verification.md)).\n3. **Strony prawne i UI istniej\u0105 w ka\u017cdym wydanym j\u0119zyku** (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n4. **Strony programatyczne potrzebuj\u0105 stra\u017cnik\u00f3w jako\u015bci** (minimum pozycji, ocen, \u017ar\u00f3de\u0142) i r\u0119cznie\n   pisanego wst\u0119pu \u2014 cienka strona generowana masowo to spam.\n5. **Tematy YMYL (zdrowie, pieni\u0105dze, bezpiecze\u0144stwo) wymagaj\u0105 E-E-A-T:** nazwany autor i operator,\n   cytowane \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142a, \u017cadnej regulowanej porady \u2014 zamiast niej disclaimer i \u015bcie\u017cka kryzysowa.\n6. **Rejestr jest cz\u0119\u015bci\u0105 t\u0142umaczenia:** poprawne, ale zimne copy to bug. T\u0142umaczenie maszynowe jest OK na\n   szkielet; **cz\u0142owiek przegl\u0105da disclaimery, komunikaty kryzysowe i teksty prawne.**\n7. **J\u0119zyki RTL lustrzanie odwracaj\u0105 ca\u0142y layout**, nie tylko tekst.\n8. **Mierz, co konwertuje** (Search Console, zanonimizowana analityka) i niech to wybiera nast\u0119pn\u0105 stron\u0119 do napisania.\n9. **Core Web Vitals to sygna\u0142 rankingowy** \u2192 mierz i poprawiaj (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Upychanie s\u0142\u00f3w kluczowych; cienkie strony programatyczne.\n- \ud83d\udeab Strona wieloj\u0119zyczna bez `hreflang`.\n- \ud83d\udeab Nieprzejrzane t\u0142umaczenie maszynowe wra\u017cliwego copy.\n- \ud83d\udeab Strona prawna tylko po angielsku w produkcie wieloj\u0119zycznym.\n", "11-data-model-and-normalization": "# 11 \u2014 Model danych i normalizacja\n\n> Przykazania V i VII u korzenia: schemat to kontrakt ca\u0142ego systemu. Najpierw normalizuj; denormalizuj\n> \u015bwiadomie, z nazwanym \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142em prawdy.\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **S\u0142owniki kontrolowane \u017cyj\u0105 w tabelach lookup** (kraje, typy, tagi) plus `CHECK` tam, gdzie warto\u015bci\n   s\u0105 sta\u0142e \u2014 nigdy wolny tekst w kolumnie.\n2. **Relacja wiele-do-wielu idzie przez tabel\u0119 \u0142\u0105cz\u0105c\u0105**, nigdy kolumny `primary_x` + `secondary_x`.\n3. **Stabilnym identyfikatorem jest slug, nie numeryczne id.** Id dryfuj\u0105 po merge'ach; dane user\u00f3w mapujesz\n   `slug \u2192 nowe id`, brakuj\u0105cy slug pomijasz i logujesz (\u2192 [19](19-database-swap-runbook.md)).\n4. **Aktywny wiersz zamiast nadpisywania tam, gdzie historia ma warto\u015b\u0107:** wyga\u015b stary wiersz (`expired_at`),\n   wstaw nowy; partial unique index (`WHERE expired_at IS NULL`) pilnuje jednego aktywnego na klucz. Audyt za darmo.\n5. **Warto\u015bci liczone s\u0105 liczone, nie zapisywane** (np. nazwa wy\u015bwietlana z cz\u0119\u015bci) \u2014 zapisane dryfuj\u0105.\n6. **Cache albo snapshot nazywa swoje \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142o prawdy** i wraca do niego, gdy jest nieaktualny; nigdy dwa \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142a jednego faktu.\n7. **Migracje s\u0105 forward-only i addytywne**, skrypty sprawdzaj\u0105 istnienie kolumny/tabeli, a ka\u017cda operacja\n   na danych ko\u0144czy si\u0119 `foreign_key_check` (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n8. **Schemat jest udokumentowany:** ERD plus dozwolone warto\u015bci ka\u017cdego pola s\u0142ownikowego, w `docs/`.\n9. **Zadeklarowanej prywatno\u015bci pilnuje model:** wra\u017cliwe przetwarzanie to osobny, idempotentny pipeline na\n   danych **zanonimizowanych**, z testem, \u017ce reidentyfikacja jest niemo\u017cliwa (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Wolny tekst tam, gdzie powinien by\u0107 lookup (\u201eUSA\", \u201eU.S.A.\", \u201eUnited States\").\n- \ud83d\udeab Jeden fakt trzymany w dw\u00f3ch miejscach bez synchronizacji.\n- \ud83d\udeab Dane user\u00f3w mapowane po zmiennym id.\n- \ud83d\udeab Zapisana warto\u015b\u0107 liczona, kt\u00f3ra dryfuje.\n- \ud83d\udeab Destrukcyjna migracja pod dzia\u0142aj\u0105cym starym kodem.\n", "12-flexibility-and-scalability": "# 12 \u2014 Elastyczno\u015b\u0107 i skalowalno\u015b\u0107\n\n> Z\u0142ota zasada altytudy w architekturze: zmiana bez przepisywania, wzrost bez przebudowy \u2014 i skalowanie\n> dopiero, gdy wymaga go metryka, nigdy \u201ena wszelki wypadek\".\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Warstwy rozdziela kontrakt, nie wsp\u00f3lny kod:** dane \u2194 ingest \u2194 web, spi\u0119te schematem\n   (\u2192 [11](11-data-model-and-normalization.md)) albo jawnym kontraktem API. Ka\u017cd\u0105 stron\u0119 da si\u0119 wtedy wymieni\u0107.\n2. **Zmiany s\u0105 addytywne i wstecznie zgodne:** nowa kolumna, endpoint czy j\u0119zyk nie psuj\u0105 tego, co dzia\u0142a.\n3. **Feature flagi wchodz\u0105 z pierwsz\u0105 ryzykown\u0105 zmian\u0105, kt\u00f3ra trafia do \u017cywych user\u00f3w** \u2014 wypu\u015b\u0107 po cichu,\n   potem rozkr\u0119caj. Nie w Dniu 0 prototypu; nigdy pomijane, gdy realni userzy s\u0105 zagro\u017ceni.\n4. **Proces bezstanowy tam, gdzie si\u0119 da**; sesje i stan id\u0105 do store'a (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n5. **Platforma to zapisany kompromis:** zawsze w\u0142\u0105czony VPS (sta\u0142y koszt, bez cold startu) vs scale-to-zero\n   (p\u0142acisz za u\u017cycie, cold start) \u2014 wybrany wg profilu ruchu i bud\u017cetu, zapisany jako ADR.\n6. **Ka\u017cdy cache ma jawn\u0105 inwalidacj\u0119** i podany czas \u017cycia.\n7. **Pipeline'y s\u0105 komponowalne i idempotentne:** ka\u017cdy etap dzia\u0142a sam, ponowny przebieg jest bezpieczny (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n8. **Produkt globalny = i18n od pierwszego commita**, nie doklejane (\u2192 [10](10-seo-and-translations.md)).\n9. **Skaluj wg liczb:** SQLite \u2192 PostgreSQL, monolit \u2192 us\u0142ugi, tylko gdy latencja, koszt albo rozmiar\n   mierzalnie tego wymagaj\u0105 \u2014 z ADR (\u2192 [13](13-performance-frontend-and-sql.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Mikroserwisy, Kafka albo Kubernetes dla stu user\u00f3w.\n- \ud83d\udeab Stan w pami\u0119ci procesu, kt\u00f3ry blokuje restarty i skalowanie.\n- \ud83d\udeab Przepisanie od zera zamiast addytywnych krok\u00f3w.\n- \ud83d\udeab Ryzykowna zmiana dla \u017cywych user\u00f3w bez flagi i bez rozkr\u0119cania.\n- \ud83d\udeab Migracja do PostgreSQL \u201ena wszelki wypadek\", gdy SQLite si\u0119 nawet nie spoci\u0142.\n", "13-performance-frontend-and-sql": "# 13 \u2014 Wydajno\u015b\u0107: frontend i SQL\n\n> Przykazanie III zastosowane do szybko\u015bci: \u201ewydaje si\u0119 szybciej\" nie istnieje \u2014 istniej\u0105 **liczby przed\n> i po**. Standardowa higiena jest za\u0142o\u017cona (indeksy na gor\u0105cych kolumnach `WHERE`/`JOIN`, brak N+1, brak\n> `SELECT *`, WebP + `srcset`, lazy loading poni\u017cej folda, fonty self-hosted z `font-display: swap`,\n> paginacja po stronie serwera).\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Najpierw mierz, potem udowodnij.** Lighthouse / Core Web Vitals (LCP, CLS, INP) dla frontu,\n   `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` dla SQL \u2014 raportowane **przed i po**. \u017badnej optymalizacji bez liczby; niezmierzona\n   \u201eoptymalizacja\" bywa regresj\u0105 w przebraniu.\n2. **Baseline Lighthouse zaraz po pierwszej dzia\u0142aj\u0105cej stronie** \u2014 inaczej nie ma do czego por\u00f3wna\u0107.\n3. **Cache-busting per deploy:** statyki s\u0105 `immutable` z d\u0142ugim `max-age`, wi\u0119c ka\u017cdy URL assetu niesie\n   `?v=<git-short-hash>` \u2014 inaczej userzy jad\u0105 na starej aplikacji a\u017c do twardego od\u015bwie\u017cenia (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n4. **Streamuj odpowiedzi LLM** token po tokenie (SSE) \u2014 nigdy pusty ekran do ko\u0144ca generacji (\u2192 [15](15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots.md)).\n5. **Drogie agregaty s\u0105 cache'owane** (snapshot statystyk z fallbackiem na \u017cyw\u0105 baz\u0119), nie liczone przy ka\u017cdym \u017c\u0105daniu.\n\n## Mobile: viewport to ruchomy cel\n\n6. **`100dvh`, nie `100vh`, dla paneli pe\u0142noekranowych** \u2014 najpierw `100vh` jako fallback, potem `100dvh`\n   (wygrywa ostatnia regu\u0142a, kt\u00f3r\u0105 silnik rozumie).\n7. **Elementy przyklejone nad klawiatur\u0105 kotwicz do `visualViewport`** (`height`/`offsetTop`, odczyt na jego\n   `resize`/`scroll`), nie `position: fixed; bottom: 0`. Skacz do nowego rozmiaru; nie animuj klawiatury.\n8. **Resetuj `flex-basis` tam, gdzie obraca si\u0119 o\u015b g\u0142\u00f3wna** \u2014 `flex: 1 1 220px` z wiersza staje si\u0119 w kolumnie\n   pustym boxem wysokim na 220px (`flex: 0 0 auto` na tym breakpoincie).\n9. **Emulator k\u0142amie:** mierz realn\u0105 geometri\u0119 (`getBoundingClientRect`) i trzymaj test na profilu urz\u0105dzenia\n   (np. projekt Playwright przypi\u0119ty do telefonu): brak poziomego overflow, kluczowy element nad foldem.\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Optymalizacja bez pomiaru.\n- \ud83d\udeab Przeliczanie `COUNT(*)` po ca\u0142ej bazie przy ka\u017cdym \u017c\u0105daniu.\n- \ud83d\udeab Blokowanie UI do czasu pe\u0142nej odpowiedzi LLM.\n- \ud83d\udeab Panele na `100vh` i inputy na `fixed; bottom: 0` na mobile.\n- \ud83d\udeab \u201eNaprawianie\" mobile ze screenshota z desktopowego emulatora.\n", "14-operational-resilience": "# 14 \u2014 Odporno\u015b\u0107 operacyjna i zewn\u0119trzne zale\u017cno\u015bci\n\n> Przykazania III, V, VI w runtime: prod \u017cyje w zawodnym \u015bwiecie. Najgorsze bugi nie krzycz\u0105 \u2014 zawieszaj\u0105,\n> wylogowuj\u0105 wszystkich albo drenuj\u0105 bud\u017cet. Prawie ka\u017cd\u0105 regu\u0142\u0119 tutaj op\u0142aci\u0142 incydent.\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Jeden z\u0142y request degraduje ten request, nie us\u0142ug\u0119:** \u0142apacze na poziomie procesu dla nieobs\u0142u\u017conych\n   rejection/wyj\u0105tk\u00f3w, trasy async przekazuj\u0105ce b\u0142\u0119dy dalej, handler 500 bez wycieku stack trace'a. Bro\u0144 si\u0119\n   przed realnymi danymi z brzegu (konto OAuth bez has\u0142a, `null` tam, gdzie zak\u0142adasz string).\n2. **D\u0142ugie zadania s\u0105 wznawialne:** checkpoint sko\u0144czonych jednostek, zapis co N, idempotencja ca\u0142o\u015bci \u2014\n   crash kosztuje ostatni batch, nie ca\u0142y przebieg.\n3. **Dopasuj runner do czasu \u017cycia zadania:** t\u0142o agenta prze\u017cywa tury, ale umiera z sesj\u0105; wszystko, co\n   ma prze\u017cy\u0107 rozmow\u0119, chodzi jako **proces odpi\u0119ty albo zadanie schedulera systemu**.\n4. **Zewn\u0119trzne \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142a s\u0105 wrogie:** timeout na ka\u017cde wywo\u0142anie, retry z wyk\u0142adniczym backoffem i limitem\n   pr\u00f3b, i **waliduj odpowied\u017a, nie status** \u2014 \u201e200 + strona b\u0142\u0119du\" to pora\u017cka.\n5. **Sie\u0107 providera psuje dzia\u0142aj\u0105cy kod dopiero na prodzie:** np. wychodz\u0105cy SMTP 465 zablokowany \u2192 587 +\n   STARTTLS z twardymi timeoutami po\u0142\u0105czenia/powitania. \u201eWys\u0142ane\" \u2260 dostarczone: zweryfikowana domena nadawcy\n   (SPF/DKIM) i realna wysy\u0142ka testowa. Sprawdzaj u providera, nie lokalnie.\n6. **Ka\u017cdy p\u0142atny zas\u00f3b stoi za tward\u0105 kwot\u0105** \u2014 per user i tier, egzekwowan\u0105 po stronie serwera, z jasnym\n   komunikatem i dat\u0105 resetu podan\u0105 z g\u00f3ry.\n7. **Wiedz, \u017ce prod \u017cyje:** logi strukturalne z poziomami (bez sekret\u00f3w, bez pe\u0142nych PII), `/healthz`,\n   zewn\u0119trzny ping uptime, alerty na to, co boli (skoki 5xx, nieudane p\u0142atno\u015bci/maile, job, kt\u00f3ry nie\n   ruszy\u0142), b\u0142\u0119dy kierowane tam, gdzie je zobaczysz.\n8. **Zadania cykliczne: pilnuj wyniku, nie runnera.** Sprawdzaj **wiek naj\u015bwie\u017cszego rekordu per \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142o**\n   (`MAX(updated_at)` grupowane po \u017ar\u00f3dle), nie kod wyj\u015bcia \u2014 zielony raport chodzi\u0142 kiedy\u015b trzy dni obok\n   44% zamro\u017conych danych. Raport pokrywa tylko \u015bcie\u017cki, o kt\u00f3rych wie.\n9. **Ustawienia schedulera to produkcja i \u017cyj\u0105 poza repo** (ponowienie po przegapionym starcie, warunki\n   zasilania i sieci, konto, katalog roboczy) \u2014 wpisz je do runbooka i sprawdzaj po ka\u017cdej zmianie systemu,\n   sprz\u0119tu albo konta.\n10. **Ka\u017cdy incydent staje si\u0119 datowan\u0105, numerowan\u0105 lekcj\u0105 w runbooku** (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Brak globalnego \u0142apacza \u2014 jeden z\u0142y request restartuje us\u0142ug\u0119 wszystkim.\n- \ud83d\udeab D\u0142ugi scraper bez checkpoint\u00f3w, odpalony z t\u0142a sesji.\n- \ud83d\udeab Zewn\u0119trzne wywo\u0142anie bez timeoutu i backoffu.\n- \ud83d\udeab P\u0142atne API bez kwoty.\n- \ud83d\udeab Sesje w pami\u0119ci procesu \u2014 ka\u017cdy deploy wylogowuje wszystkich.\n- \ud83d\udeab Zadanie cykliczne pilnowane w\u0142asnym kodem wyj\u015bcia.\n- \ud83d\udeab Sekrety albo PII w logach.\n", "15-scraping-ai-and-chatbots": "# 15 \u2014 Scraping, API AI i konfigurowalne chatboty\n\n> Dane z sieci i AI jako funkcje produktu \u2014 skutecznie, tanio i **na kontrakcie**. Warstwa operacyjna\n> (timeouty, backoff, kwoty, wznawialno\u015b\u0107) \u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md).\n\n## Scraping\n\n1. **Najpierw oficjalne \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142o** (API, feed, eksport, sitemap); scraping HTML to ostateczno\u015b\u0107.\n2. **Szanuj \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142o:** `robots.txt`/regulamin, rozs\u0105dny throttle, prawdziwy User-Agent (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n3. **Strona odpowiada dwa razy \u2014 polityk\u0105 i maszyneri\u0105.** Gdy `robots.txt` pozwala na odczyt, a tarcza\n   antybotowa blokuje, przedstaw si\u0119 i **zapytaj w\u0142a\u015bciciela**; nigdy nie buduj obej\u015bcia tarczy. Je\u015bli pro\u015bba\n   nie przejdzie, \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142o wypada z produktu.\n4. **Odr\u00f3\u017cnij blokad\u0119 IP od tarczy, zanim cokolwiek zbudujesz:** 403 tutaj, a 200 z innej sieci = zablokowany\n   adres (przenie\u015b pobieranie); 403 z ka\u017cdej sieci = tarcza jest sednem.\n5. **Waliduj kszta\u0142t wyniku**, nie status \u2014 dryf selektora podnosi alarm, nigdy ciche zera.\n6. **Normalizuj u wej\u015bcia** wzgl\u0119dem tabel lookup (\u2192 [11](11-data-model-and-normalization.md)); pobieraj\n   **delt\u0119**, nie ca\u0142y katalog; loadery s\u0105 idempotentne (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n\n## API AI\n\n7. **LLM tylko tam, gdzie deterministyczny kod nie daje rady** (ekstrakcja z nieustrukturyzowanego tekstu,\n   klasyfikacja, streszczenia) \u2014 nigdy do tego, co zrobi regex, SQL albo funkcja.\n8. **Wymu\u015b kontrakt wyj\u015bcia:** JSON schema / tool use, walidacja, ponowienie przy niezgodno\u015bci. Wyj\u015bcie LLM to niezaufane wej\u015bcie.\n9. **Cache dla identycznych wej\u015b\u0107; kwota na ka\u017cde p\u0142atne wywo\u0142anie; wzbogacaj offline w batchu\n   z checkpointami**, a nie na \u017cywo w \u017c\u0105daniu usera. Mierz koszt i latencj\u0119 per zadanie.\n\n## Chatboty\n\n10. **Persona, zakres i regu\u0142y to konfiguracja** (wersjonowane dane), nie kod \u2014 strojone bez deployu.\n11. **Ugruntowane w Twoich danych, ze \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142em**; bezpieczne \u201enie wiem\" zamiast zmy\u015blania.\n12. **Tre\u015b\u0107 usera i z sieci to dane, nigdy instrukcje** (prompt injection): \u017cadnej akcji ze skutkami bez\n    potwierdzenia, \u017cadnego wycieku system promptu ani sekret\u00f3w.\n13. **Z\u0142oty zestaw pyta\u0144 chodzi jak testy** przy ka\u017cdej zmianie promptu.\n14. **Disclaimer i podstawa prawna dla przechowywanych rozm\u00f3w** (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Scraping, gdy istnieje API; pe\u0142ny re-scrape co noc.\n- \ud83d\udeab Obchodzenie tarczy antybotowej, kt\u00f3r\u0105 postawi\u0142 w\u0142a\u015bciciel.\n- \ud83d\udeab Parsowanie wolnej prozy i nadzieja na format.\n- \ud83d\udeab Brak cache'u i kwoty na p\u0142atnych wywo\u0142aniach.\n- \ud83d\udeab Persona wbita w kod; nieugruntowany asystent.\n- \ud83d\udeab Akcja na podstawie wiadomo\u015bci z czatu bez potwierdzenia.\n", "16-driving-claude": "# 16 \u2014 Prowadzenie Claude\n\n> Funkcje agenta dokumentuje sam agent; ten rozdzia\u0142 ustala tylko **polityk\u0119 projektu** ich u\u017cycia \u2014 kiedy\n> s\u0105 obowi\u0105zkowe i gdzie s\u0105 granice.\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Plan mode jest obowi\u0105zkowy**, gdy kszta\u0142t pracy jest otwarty, promie\u0144 ra\u017cenia szeroki (migracja, rename\n   po ca\u0142ym drzewie, URL-e/redirecty) albo zmian\u0119 trudno cofn\u0105\u0107. Plan nazywa pliki, kompromis i **jak wynik\n   zostanie zweryfikowany**. Przy ograniczonym fixie \u2014 odpu\u015b\u0107.\n2. **Autopilot to default \u2014 w granicach mapy uprawnie\u0144** (\u2192 [06](06-collaboration-and-memory.md)). \u015arodkowa\n   i prawa kolumna wci\u0105\u017c go zatrzymuj\u0105; to w\u0142a\u015bnie czyni autopilota bezpiecznym.\n3. **Model wg stawki:** ta\u0144szy do pracy mechanicznej, najmocniejszy do architektury, trudnych bug\u00f3w i\n   wszystkiego, co mo\u017ce zgubi\u0107 dane. Nigdy nie oszcz\u0119dzaj na operacji, kt\u00f3ra mo\u017ce zgubi\u0107 dane.\n4. **D\u0142uga praca w tle; to, co ma prze\u017cy\u0107 sesj\u0119 \u2014 odpi\u0119te** (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n5. **Rozpraszaj na subagent\u00f3w**, gdy realnie pomaga szeroko\u015b\u0107, r\u00f3wnoleg\u0142e niezale\u017cne edycje albo\n   adwersarialna weryfikacja \u2014 zostaje wniosek, nie zrzuty. Nie do poprawki na jedn\u0105 lini\u0119.\n6. **Jedna r\u00f3wnoleg\u0142a sesja = jeden `git worktree`.** Dwie sesje w jednym checkoucie dziel\u0105 index:\n   stageuj\u0105 sobie pliki i wmiataj\u0105 p\u00f3\u0142-gotowe edycje do commit\u00f3w (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n7. **Powtarzalne procedury staj\u0105 si\u0119 skillami; \u201ezawsze r\u00f3b X po Y\" staje si\u0119 hookiem** (\u2192 [02](02-skills-and-refactoring.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Szeroka albo nieodwracalna zmiana bez plan mode \u2014 recenzujesz diff zamiast decyzji.\n- \ud83d\udeab R\u0119czne potwierdzanie ka\u017cdej bezpiecznej edycji \u2014 wolno i uczy przyklepywania.\n- \ud83d\udeab Tani model na migracji, kt\u00f3ra mo\u017ce zgubi\u0107 dane.\n- \ud83d\udeab Dwie sesje w jednym checkoucie.\n- \ud83d\udeab Zadanie, kt\u00f3re ma prze\u017cy\u0107 sesj\u0119, zostawione w tle sesji.\n", "17-language-and-guards": "# 17 \u2014 J\u0119zyk kodu i stra\u017cnicy, kt\u00f3rzy trzymaj\u0105 regu\u0142y przy \u017cyciu\n\n> Przykazania I i III zastosowane do nazw: regu\u0142a, kt\u00f3ra \u017cyje tylko w czyjej\u015b g\u0142owie, nie jest regu\u0142\u0105,\n> a regu\u0142a bez testu to \u017cyczenie.\n\n## Dwa j\u0119zyki, podzielone wg czytelnika\n\n| Warstwa | J\u0119zyk |\n|---|---|\n| **Kod** \u2014 identyfikatory (modu\u0142y, klasy, funkcje, zmienne, fixture'y, **nazwy test\u00f3w**), nazwy plik\u00f3w kodu, tabele i kolumny bazy, flagi CLI, klucze configu i API, warto\u015bci enum\u00f3w/stan\u00f3w, klucze log\u00f3w, opisy commit\u00f3w | **angielski, zawsze** |\n| **Proza** \u2014 docsy, plany, `AI_README`, komentarze, docstringi, tekst dla usera | **j\u0119zyk projektu**, zadeklarowany raz w `CLAUDE.md` |\n\n```\n# CLAUDE.md\nCode: English (identifiers, tests, DB, CLI, keys). Prose: Polish (docs, comments, docstrings, UI).\n```\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Angielski kod to j\u0119zyk ekosystemu** \u2014 narz\u0119dzia, autouzupe\u0142nianie, b\u0142\u0119dy i nast\u0119pny in\u017cynier m\u00f3wi\u0105 nim;\n   identyfikator w j\u0119zyku natywnym transliteruje si\u0119 na trzy r\u00f3\u017cne sposoby.\n2. **Testy to kod.** `test_<podmiot>_<warunek>_<oczekiwanie>` po angielsku; *dlaczego* (incydent, data, koszt)\n   trafia do docstringa, w j\u0119zyku prozy.\n3. **\u017badnych mieszanych identyfikator\u00f3w** (`test_cost_NIE_ISTNIEJE_in_window`) \u2014 nieczytelne i niegrepowalne.\n4. **Nazwy, kt\u00f3re drukuje narz\u0119dzie, zostaj\u0105 po angielsku wewn\u0105trz prozy** \u2014 komenda, flaga, status, plik\n   albo tag (`commit`, `--dry-run`, `migrate.py`, `deploy-*`): to ich szukasz grepem. Zwyk\u0142e s\u0142owo na sam\u0105\n   czynno\u015b\u0107 mo\u017ce by\u0107 w j\u0119zyku prozy (\u201ewdro\u017cenie\" na deploy).\n5. **Regu\u0142a bez stra\u017cnika to \u017cyczenie.** Jedna sesja wypu\u015bci\u0142a 95 z 98 commit\u00f3w bez dotkni\u0119cia indeksu,\n   kt\u00f3ry mia\u0142a aktualizowa\u0107. Regu\u0142y, kt\u00f3re maj\u0105 prze\u017cy\u0107 d\u0142ug\u0105 sesj\u0119, egzekwuje suite test\u00f3w.\n\n## Dwa stra\u017cniki (gotowe w `guards/`)\n\nSkopiuj je do `tests/` projektu jako `test_ai_readme.py` i `test_code_language.py`, dopasuj listy katalog\u00f3w,\nodpal, zacommituj baseline \u2014 w **pierwszym commicie** (\u2192 [07](07-new-project-day-0.md)).\nTo **referencyjne implementacje w pyte\u015bcie**: projekt w Pythonie je kopiuje; projekt w Go, Rust, Kotlinie albo\nTypeScripcie przenosi t\u0119 sam\u0105 specyfikacj\u0119 do w\u0142asnego test runnera (stra\u017cnik indeksu potrzebuje tylko glob\u00f3w\nplik\u00f3w; stra\u017cnik j\u0119zyka \u2014 parsera identyfikator\u00f3w albo lintera danego j\u0119zyka). Regu\u0142\u0105 jest specyfikacja, nie plik w Pythonie.\n\n- **[`guards/ai_readme_guard.py`](../guards/ai_readme_guard.py)** \u2014 ka\u017cdy katalog z kodem ma `AI_README.md`;\n  **ka\u017cdy modu\u0142 jest w nim wymieniony**; **sufit 20 kB** (powy\u017cej indeks przestaje by\u0107 indeksem \u2014 dziel\n  katalog, nie plik); lista pilnowanych katalog\u00f3w pilnuje sama siebie. Nie oceni, czy opis jest *aktualny* \u2014\n  to zostaje obowi\u0105zkiem pisz\u0105cego.\n- **[`guards/code_language_guard.py`](../guards/code_language_guard.py)** \u2014 oznacza identyfikatory wygl\u0105daj\u0105ce\n  na j\u0119zyk prozy (stop-lista s\u0142\u00f3w funkcyjnych + kszta\u0142t liter); **zapadka** z baselinem per plik.\n\n## Zapadka \u2014 jak wprowadzi\u0107 dowoln\u0105 regu\u0142\u0119 do starego kodu\n\n1. **Zamro\u017a** dzisiejsz\u0105 liczb\u0119 per plik w zacommitowanym baselinie (w nowym projekcie pusty).\n2. **Nowe pliki musz\u0105 by\u0107 czyste** od dnia, w kt\u00f3rym regu\u0142a powsta\u0142a.\n3. **Liczby mog\u0105 tylko spada\u0107;** gdy spadnie, obni\u017c baseline **na polecenie** (`--write-baseline`), nigdy po cichu.\n4. **R\u0119czne podniesienie baseline'u to os\u0142abienie stra\u017cnika** \u2014 ten sam grzech co pomini\u0119cie testu.\n5. **Wyj\u0105tki s\u0105 datowane**, ograniczone do dzisiejszego rozmiaru i powi\u0105zane z zadaniem w backlogu, kt\u00f3re je usuwa.\n\nWpinasz doktryn\u0119 do istniej\u0105cego projektu: odpal stra\u017cnika j\u0119zyka raz, zacommituj baseline taki, jaki jest,\ni wpisz do backlogu \u201eprzemianuj obce identyfikatory \u2014 zosta\u0142o N\", z liczb\u0105.\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab \u201eNazwy test\u00f3w to nie kod.\"\n- \ud83d\udeab Identyfikatory mieszaj\u0105ce j\u0119zyki.\n- \ud83d\udeab T\u0142umaczenie nazwy, kt\u00f3r\u0105 drukuje narz\u0119dzie (`--dry-run` jako \u201epr\u00f3ba\").\n- \ud83d\udeab Regu\u0142a w `CLAUDE.md` bez testu za ni\u0105.\n- \ud83d\udeab Podniesienie baseline'u, \u017ceby suite by\u0142 zielony.\n- \ud83d\udeab `AI_README` ponad sufitem, \u201ebo tu wszystko jest wa\u017cne\".\n", "18-release-rehearsal": "# 18 \u2014 Pr\u00f3ba generalna wydania: dev \u2192 preprod \u2192 prod\n\n> Przykazania V i VI: migracja albo swap bazy odpalony **pierwszy raz** w prodowym oknie maintenance jest\n> testowany na Twoich userach. Najpierw odpal dok\u0142adnie ten runbook raz na wiernej kopii.\n\n## Trzy \u015brodowiska, trzy zadania\n\n| \u015arodowisko | Zadanie | Dane |\n|---|---|---|\n| **dev** (Twoja maszyna) | budowanie, odtwarzanie bug\u00f3w | dane seed albo **zanonimizowany** refresh z backupu proda |\n| **preprod** (serwerowy klon proda: ten sam system, serwer WWW, mened\u017cer proces\u00f3w, TLS) | pr\u00f3ba migracji i samego deployu | zanonimizowany backup proda; **za basic-auth, `noindex`** |\n| **prod** | obs\u0142uga user\u00f3w | \u015bwi\u0119ty \u2014 dotykany tylko na \u201ewdra\u017caj\", tylko krokami prze\u0107wiczonymi tego samego dnia na preprodzie |\n\nPreprod dok\u0142adasz, gdy projekt wchodzi na trzeci szczebel branchowania albo przy pierwszej ryzykownej migracji (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n\n## Regu\u0142y\n\n1. **Buduj preprod z proda za ka\u017cdym razem tak samo \u2014 skryptem:** klon kluczem deployowym proda; refresh bazy\n   z backupu proda; **anonimizuj PII, zanim opuszcz\u0105 proda** (\u2192 [09](09-law-and-protecting-the-creator.md)).\n2. **Warto\u015bci zale\u017cne od \u015brodowiska s\u0105 nadpisywalne przez env, nigdy wpisane na sztywno:** `SITE_URL`,\n   canonical, OG, sitemap wskazuj\u0105 na preprod \u2014 inaczej preprod przecieka prodowymi URL-ami albo trafia do indeksu (\u2192 [10](10-seo-and-translations.md)).\n3. **\u0106wicz prawdziwy runbook, nie uproszczony:** odpal migracj\u0119, zmierz okno, zr\u00f3b smoke, potwierd\u017a, \u017ce jest\n   forward-only i idempotentna (\u2192 [04](04-scripts-and-databases.md)).\n4. **Spodziewaj si\u0119 pu\u0142apek, kt\u00f3re ujawnia tylko prawdziwy preprod:** wyzwanie ACME/Let's Encrypt `http-01`\n   musi omija\u0107 basic-auth, inaczej odnowienie certu pada z 401; prywatne repo potrzebuje zdalnego SSH przed pierwszym pullem.\n5. **Preprod to scena pr\u00f3b, nigdy drugi prod:** bez \u017cywych user\u00f3w, nie\u015bwie\u017ca baza jest OK, a **jego bazy nigdy\n   nie podmienia si\u0119 z powrotem na prod** (\u2192 [11](11-data-model-and-normalization.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Migracja, kt\u00f3rej pierwszym przebiegiem jest prodowe okno maintenance.\n- \ud83d\udeab Realne, niezanonimizowane dane user\u00f3w na preprodzie \u2014 druga kopia odpowiedzialno\u015bci za PII na s\u0142abszym boxie.\n- \ud83d\udeab Preprod zaindeksowany albo emituj\u0105cy prodowe URL-e.\n- \ud83d\udeab Traktowanie danych preprodu jako \u017ar\u00f3d\u0142a prawdy.\n", "19-database-swap-runbook": "# 19 \u2014 Runbook swapu bazy\n\n> Przykazanie VII w najtrudniejszej postaci: wymiana ca\u0142ej bazy proda (np. przebudowanego katalogu) **z\n> zachowaniem ka\u017cdego \u017cywego konta i wszystkiego, co userzy stworzyli.** Rzadkie \u2014 i to tu le\u017cy najwi\u0119cej pu\u0142apek.\n\n## Regu\u0142y, kt\u00f3re ratuj\u0105 dane user\u00f3w\n\n1. **\u201eUserzy\" to wiele tabel.** Wylicz **ka\u017cd\u0105** tabel\u0119 z kluczem obcym do usera (konta, koszyki, recenzje,\n   polubienia, odznaki, sesje, sesje czatu + wiadomo\u015bci + limity, feedback\u2026). Tabel\u0119 bez `user_id` mapujesz\n   przez jej rodzica. Ka\u017cd\u0105 bramkuj istnieniem tabeli/kolumny.\n2. **Mapuj po stabilnym kluczu (slug \u2192 nowe id), nigdy po starym id.** Brakuj\u0105cy slug pomijasz i logujesz.\n3. **\u0179r\u00f3d\u0142em prawdy dla kont jest \u017cywy prod**, czytany po `stop` w oknie maintenance \u2014 nie starszy snapshot,\n   kt\u00f3ry gubi rejestracje z ostatniej godziny.\n4. **Kolejno\u015b\u0107 bezpieczna dla FK:** usuwaj dzieci przed rodzicami, wstawiaj rodzic\u00f3w przed dzie\u0107mi.\n5. **Backup \u017cywego proda tu\u017c przed swapem** (`.backup replaced-prod-<ts>.db`) \u2014 to jest rollback.\n6. **Zweryfikuj scalon\u0105 baz\u0119:** liczba kont = prod, 0 wisz\u0105cych odwo\u0142a\u0144, `integrity_check: ok`, naruszenia FK\n   nie wy\u017cej ni\u017c stan zastany.\n7. **Sesje \u017cyj\u0105 we w\u0142asnym store** (np. `sessions.db`), nigdy w podmienianej bazie i nigdy w pami\u0119ci procesu \u2014\n   inaczej swap albo dowolny restart wylogowuje wszystkich (\u2192 [14](14-operational-resilience.md)).\n\n## Sekwencja\n\n1. **Faza na \u017cywo:** wypchnij kod; skopiuj gitignorowane assety **przed** oknem (rsync / tar po ssh, tylko\n   delta) \u2014 nie jad\u0105 przez `git pull`; wgraj nowy snapshot na serwer.\n2. **Okno (sekundy, atomowo, `set -e`):** flaga ON \u2192 pull \u2192 stop \u2192 backup \u017cywego proda \u2192 migracja user\u00f3w\n   (\u017ar\u00f3d\u0142o = prod) \u2192 podmiana pliku (usu\u0144 `-wal`/`-shm`, przenie\u015b, w\u0142\u0105cz z powrotem WAL) \u2192 start \u2192 **smoke** \u2192 flaga OFF.\n3. **Smoke przez `localhost`** (omija stron\u0119 maintenance): kluczowe strony 200, redirecty, liczby wierszy, czyste\n   logi procesu. Trywialny, bezpieczny bug znaleziony w oknie naprawiasz w oknie \u2014 nie wypuszczaj znanego 500.\n4. **Po:** otaguj deploy, posprz\u0105taj pliki tymczasowe, wpisz lekcje do runbooka (\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n\n## Antywzorce\n- \ud83d\udeab Swap \u201epr\u00f3cz kont\" trzymaj\u0105cy tylko tabel\u0119 `users` \u2014 recenzje, odznaki i czat przepadaj\u0105.\n- \ud83d\udeab Mapowanie danych user\u00f3w po id \u2014 recenzje l\u0105duj\u0105 na z\u0142ym produkcie.\n- \ud83d\udeab Migracja kont ze starego snapshotu.\n- \ud83d\udeab Przenoszenie gigabajt\u00f3w asset\u00f3w w trakcie okna.\n- \ud83d\udeab `scp` \u017cywej `.db` \u2014 u\u017cyj `.backup`; WAL trzyma \u015bwie\u017ce strony.\n", "intro": "# Czym jest Rzemios\u0142o \u2014 The Craft\n\n> **Zestaw twardych regu\u0142 budowania i prowadzenia aplikacji end-to-end z Claude** \u2014 \u017ceby projekt\n> vibe-codingowy zosta\u0142 zrozumia\u0142y, testowalny, bezpieczny w zmianie i godny zaufania, od pierwszego commita\n> po prod z realnymi userami. Nie framework, nie biblioteka: doktryna, kt\u00f3r\u0105 agent czyta i kt\u00f3rej przestrzega.\n\n## Dla kogo to jest\n\nDla **pojedynczego developera**, kt\u00f3ry buduje realn\u0105 aplikacj\u0119 **z AI jako par\u0105** \u2014 nie dla korporacyjnego\nzespo\u0142u z osobnym ops i QA. \u201eSolo\" to i tak wielu pisz\u0105cych: odpalisz **kilka sesji Claude** na jednym repo,\nwi\u0119c regu\u0142y koordynacji (issues, kr\u00f3tkie ga\u0142\u0119zie, dyscyplina push/pull) licz\u0105 si\u0119 od drugiej sesji\n(\u2192 [05](05-git-and-deployments.md)).\n\n## Jak zacz\u0105\u0107\n\n1. Zainstaluj **Claude Code** (desktop albo CLI).\n2. Wype\u0142nij [brief](index.html#brief) \u2014 pyta o to, co zmienia architektur\u0119: j\u0119zyki, MVP, monetyzacj\u0119, ton.\n3. Do\u0142\u0105cz The Craft do projektu jako `docs/rules/` i zacznij od [Dnia 0](07-new-project-day-0.md).\n\nNiczego nie musisz pami\u0119ta\u0107 \u2014 regu\u0142y czyta agent. Je\u015bli czytasz jeden plik, czytaj [Dekalog](00-commandments.md).\n\nTe strony s\u0105 celowo zwi\u0119z\u0142e. **Wyja\u015bnienia, przyk\u0142ady i historie stoj\u0105ce za ka\u017cd\u0105 regu\u0142\u0105:**\n[thecraft.jakub.solutions](https://thecraft.jakub.solutions).\n"}};
